(window.LITUK_BANKS = window.LITUK_BANKS || []).push({
  id: "toughest",
  label: "Toughest Tests",
  source: "Britizen Study Guide",
  sourceUrl: "https://britizen.uk/study-guide/",
  passmark: 18,
  perTest: 24,
  tests: [
    {"n":501,"name":"Toughest Test 1","q":[{"g":3198,"t":"Which of these marks the beginning of British history?","e":"The people of the Iron Age made the first coins to be minted in Britain, some inscribed with the names of Iron Age kings. This marks the beginnings of British history.","p":1,"o":[["The first coins minted in Britain, inscribed with the names of Iron Age kings",1],["The Roman invasion led by the Emperor Claudius",0],["The building of Stonehenge in Wiltshire",0],["The arrival of the first farmers from south-east Europe",0]]},{"g":3199,"t":"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC. What was the result?","e":"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC. This was unsuccessful and for nearly 100 years Britain remained separate from the Roman Empire. In AD 43 the Emperor Claudius led the Roman army in a new invasion.","p":1,"o":[["It was unsuccessful, and Britain stayed outside the Empire for nearly 100 years",1],["Britain was occupied and Hadrian's Wall was built",0],["The Iceni surrendered and Boudicca was killed",0],["Almost all of Britain was occupied at once",0]]},{"g":3200,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nHadrian's Wall was built in the north of England to keep out the Vikings.","e":"Areas of what is now Scotland were never conquered by the Romans, and the Emperor Hadrian built a wall in the north of England to keep out the Picts, the ancestors of the Scottish people. The Vikings did not arrive until AD 789.","p":1,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3201,"t":"Which TWO forts are named in the handbook as part of Hadrian's Wall?","e":"Included in the wall were a number of forts. Parts of Hadrian's Wall, including the forts of Housesteads and Vindolanda, can still be seen. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","p":1,"o":[["Housesteads",1],["Vindolanda",1],["Maiden Castle",0],["Sutton Hoo",0]]},{"g":3202,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"St Columba founded a monastery on the island of Iona, off the coast of what is now Scotland. St Augustine led missionaries from Rome and became the first Archbishop of Canterbury.","p":1,"o":[["St Columba founded a monastery on the island of Iona",1],["St Augustine founded a monastery on the island of Iona",0]]},{"g":3203,"t":"In which year did the Roman army leave Britain?","e":"The Roman army left Britain in AD 410 to defend other parts of the Roman Empire and never returned. The Romans had remained in Britain for 400 years.","p":1,"o":[["AD 410",1],["AD 43",0],["AD 600",0],["AD 789",0]]},{"g":3204,"t":"The Vikings first visited Britain in AD 789. Why did they come?","e":"The Vikings came from Denmark and Norway. They first visited Britain in AD 789 to raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves. Later they began to stay and form their own communities.","p":1,"o":[["To raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves",1],["To trade wool with the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms",0],["To spread Christianity in the north of Britain",0],["To escape religious persecution in Denmark",0]]},{"g":3205,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe Domesday Book recorded who owned the land and what animals they owned.","e":"William sent people all over England to draw up lists of all the towns and villages. The people who lived there, who owned the land and what animals they owned were also listed. This was called the Domesday Book.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3206,"t":"What is the capital city of Northern Ireland?","e":"The capital city of the UK is London. The capital city of Scotland is Edinburgh, the capital city of Wales is Cardiff, and the capital city of Northern Ireland is Belfast.","p":0,"o":[["Belfast",1],["Cardiff",0],["Edinburgh",0],["Dundee",0]]},{"g":3207,"t":"Which TWO of these are Crown dependencies rather than parts of the UK?","e":"There are several islands which are closely linked with the UK but are not part of it: the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man. These have their own governments and are called 'Crown dependencies'.","p":4,"o":[["The Channel Islands",1],["The Isle of Man",1],["The Falkland Islands",0],["St Helena",0]]},{"g":3208,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"'Great Britain' refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland. The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.","p":4,"o":[["'Great Britain' refers only to England, Scotland and Wales",1],["'Great Britain' refers to England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland",0]]},{"g":3209,"t":"According to the handbook, what proportion of the UK population lives in England?","e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8% and Northern Ireland less than 3%.","p":0,"o":[["84%",1],["70%",0],["94%",0],["76%",0]]},{"g":3210,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nNorthern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, and shops throughout the UK are obliged to accept them.","e":"Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shops and businesses do not have to accept them.","p":0,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3211,"t":"Which country celebrates its patron saint's day on 1 March?","e":"Each patron saint has a special day: 1 March St David's Day, Wales; 17 March St Patrick's Day, Northern Ireland; 23 April St George's Day, England; 30 November St Andrew's Day, Scotland.","p":0,"o":[["Wales",1],["England",0],["Scotland",0],["Northern Ireland",0]]},{"g":3212,"t":"Which TWO patron saints' days are official holidays?","e":"Only Scotland and Northern Ireland have their patron saint's day as an official holiday, although in Scotland not all businesses and offices will close. St Patrick is the patron saint of Northern Ireland and St Andrew of Scotland.","p":0,"o":[["St Patrick's Day",1],["St Andrew's Day",1],["St George's Day",0],["St David's Day",0]]},{"g":3213,"t":"Which festival is celebrated by both Hindus and Sikhs?","e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days. It is often called the Festival of Lights. It is celebrated by Hindus and Sikhs. There is a famous celebration of Diwali in Leicester.","p":0,"o":[["Diwali",1],["Hannukah",0],["Eid al-Fitr",0],["Vaisakhi",0]]},{"g":3214,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Eid al-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month. Eid ul Adha remembers that the prophet Ibrahim was willing to sacrifice his son when God ordered him to.","p":0,"o":[["Eid al-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan",1],["Eid ul Adha celebrates the end of Ramadan",0]]},{"g":3215,"t":"What is a constitution?","e":"A constitution is a set of principles by which a country is governed. It includes all of the institutions that are responsible for running the country and how their power is kept in check. The constitution also includes laws and conventions.","p":2,"o":[["A set of principles by which a country is governed",1],["A written list of the rights of citizens only",0],["The set of laws passed by Parliament each year",0],["An agreement between the monarch and the Prime Minister",0]]},{"g":3216,"t":"Why is the British constitution described as 'unwritten'?","e":"The British constitution is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as 'unwritten'. This is mainly because the UK, unlike America or France, has never had a revolution which led permanently to a totally new system of government.","p":2,"o":[["It is not written down in any single document",1],["No part of it has ever been written down",0],["It is written only in Latin",0],["Only the monarch is permitted to read it",0]]},{"g":3217,"t":"How is the Speaker of the House of Commons chosen?","e":"Debates in the House of Commons are chaired by the Speaker. The Speaker is neutral and does not represent a political party, even though he or she is an MP. The Speaker is chosen by other MPs in a secret ballot.","p":2,"o":[["By other MPs in a secret ballot",1],["By the Prime Minister",0],["By the monarch on the advice of the cabinet",0],["By the members of the House of Lords",0]]},{"g":3218,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nMembers of the House of Lords are elected by the people and represent a constituency.","e":"Members of the House of Lords, known as peers, are not elected by the people and do not represent a constituency. Since 1958 the Prime Minister has had the power to nominate peers just for their own lifetime.","p":2,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3219,"t":"Which cabinet minister is responsible for crime, policing and immigration?","e":"The Home Secretary is responsible for crime, policing and immigration. The Chancellor of the Exchequer is responsible for the economy and the Foreign Secretary for managing relationships with foreign countries.","p":2,"o":[["The Home Secretary",1],["The Foreign Secretary",0],["The Chancellor of the Exchequer",0],["The Lord Chancellor",0]]},{"g":3220,"t":"Which of these is NOT one of the fundamental principles of British life listed in the handbook?","e":"The fundamental principles of British life include democracy, the rule of law, individual liberty, tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, and participation in community life. There is no place in British society for extremism or intolerance.","p":3,"o":[["Compulsory military service",1],["Individual liberty",0],["The rule of law",0],["Participation in community life",0]]},{"g":3221,"t":"Which TWO things does the UK offer in return, according to the handbook?","e":"In return, the UK offers freedom of belief and religion, freedom of speech, freedom from unfair discrimination, a right to a fair trial, and a right to join in the election of a government.","p":3,"o":[["A right to a fair trial",1],["Freedom from unfair discrimination",1],["A guaranteed job",0],["Free housing for all residents",0]]}]},
    {"n":502,"name":"Toughest Test 2","q":[{"g":3222,"t":"In 1284 King Edward I introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan. What did it do?","e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England. Huge castles, including Conwy and Caernarvon, were built to maintain this power.","p":1,"o":[["It annexed Wales to the Crown of England",1],["It annexed Scotland to the Crown of England",0],["It gave Wales its own parliament",0],["It ended the Hundred Years War",0]]},{"g":3223,"t":"Which battle, fought in 1314, left Scotland unconquered by the English?","e":"In Scotland, the English kings were less successful. In 1314 the Scottish, led by Robert the Bruce, defeated the English at the Battle of Bannockburn, and Scotland remained unconquered by the English.","p":1,"o":[["The Battle of Bannockburn",1],["The Battle of Agincourt",0],["The Battle of Bosworth Field",0],["The Battle of Hastings",0]]},{"g":3224,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe Hundred Years War actually lasted 116 years.","e":"English kings also fought a long war with France, called the Hundred Years War, even though it actually lasted 116 years. One of the most famous battles was Agincourt in 1415.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3225,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In 1348 a disease, probably a form of plague, came to Britain. This was known as the Black Death. One third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales.","p":1,"o":[["The Black Death killed about one third of the population of England",1],["The Black Death killed about two thirds of the population of England",0]]},{"g":3226,"t":"What was the effect of the Black Death on the area of Ireland controlled by the English?","e":"In Ireland, the Black Death killed many in the Pale and, for a time, the area controlled by the English became smaller.","p":1,"o":[["The Pale became smaller for a time",1],["The Pale doubled in size",0],["The English left Ireland completely",0],["The Pale was renamed Dublin",0]]},{"g":3227,"t":"Which TWO groups sat in the House of Lords when Parliament divided into two Houses?","e":"The nobility, great landowners and bishops sat in the House of Lords. Knights, who were usually smaller landowners, and wealthy people from towns and cities were elected to sit in the House of Commons.","p":1,"o":[["Great landowners",1],["Bishops",1],["Knights",0],["Wealthy people from towns and cities",0]]},{"g":3228,"t":"How many Houses, called Estates, did the Scottish Parliament have in the Middle Ages?","e":"A similar Parliament developed in Scotland. It had three Houses, called Estates: the lords, the commons and the clergy.","p":1,"o":[["Three",1],["Two",0],["Four",0],["One",0]]},{"g":3229,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nIn Scotland the legal system developed by codifying laws, that is, writing them down.","e":"In England, judges developed 'common law' by a process of precedence, that is following previous decisions, and tradition. In Scotland, the legal system developed slightly differently and laws were 'codified', that is, written down.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3230,"t":"Which TWO modern English words are given in the handbook as coming from Norman French?","e":"Some words in modern English, for example 'park' and 'beauty', are based on Norman French words. Others, for example 'apple', 'cow' and 'summer', are based on Anglo-Saxon words.","p":1,"o":[["Park",1],["Beauty",1],["Apple",0],["Summer",0]]},{"g":3231,"t":"Who wrote The Bruce, a poem in the Scots language about the Battle of Bannockburn?","e":"In Scotland, many people continued to speak Gaelic and the Scots language also developed. A number of poets began to write in the Scots language. One example is John Barbour, who wrote The Bruce about the Battle of Bannockburn.","p":1,"o":[["John Barbour",1],["Geoffrey Chaucer",0],["William Caxton",0],["Robert Burns",0]]},{"g":3232,"t":"What is the currency of the UK, and how many pence are in a pound?","e":"The currency in the UK is the pound sterling, symbol £. There are 100 pence in a pound. Coins are 1p, 2p, 5p, 10p, 20p, 50p, £1 and £2; notes are £5, £10, £20 and £50.","p":0,"o":[["The pound sterling, with 100 pence in a pound",1],["The pound sterling, with 50 pence in a pound",0],["The euro, with 100 cents in a euro",0],["The pound sterling, with 20 pence in a pound",0]]},{"g":3233,"t":"Which of these is NOT a denomination of UK banknote listed in the handbook?","e":"The denominations of currency are coins: 1p, 2p, 5p, 10p, 20p, 50p, £1 and £2; and notes: £5, £10, £20 and £50.","p":0,"o":[["£100",1],["£5",0],["£20",0],["£50",0]]},{"g":3234,"t":"Where in the UK is Gaelic spoken?","e":"In Scotland, Gaelic, again a different language, is spoken in some parts of the Highlands and Islands, and in Northern Ireland some people speak Irish Gaelic. In Wales, many people speak Welsh.","p":0,"o":[["In some parts of the Highlands and Islands of Scotland",1],["Throughout Wales",0],["In the south-west of England",0],["In the Channel Islands",0]]},{"g":3235,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nAccording to the handbook, nearly 10% of the population has a parent or grandparent born outside the UK.","e":"The UK today is a more diverse society than it was 100 years ago, in both ethnic and religious terms. Post-war immigration means that nearly 10% of the population has a parent or grandparent born outside the UK.","p":0,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3236,"t":"Which TWO of these cities are in Scotland, according to the handbook?","e":"The handbook lists major cities of the UK. In Scotland these include Edinburgh, Glasgow, Dundee and Aberdeen. Norwich and Plymouth are listed among the cities of England.","p":0,"o":[["Dundee",1],["Aberdeen",1],["Norwich",0],["Plymouth",0]]},{"g":3237,"t":"Which church is the established Church of the state in England?","e":"In England, there is a constitutional link between Church and state. The official Church of the state is the Church of England, called the Anglican Church in other countries and the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States. It is a Protestant Church.","p":0,"o":[["The Church of England",1],["The Church of Scotland",0],["The Roman Catholic Church",0],["The Methodist Church",0]]},{"g":3238,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"The monarch is the head of the Church of England. The spiritual leader of the Church of England is the Archbishop of Canterbury.","p":0,"o":[["The monarch is the head of the Church of England",1],["The Archbishop of Canterbury is the head of the Church of England",0]]},{"g":3239,"t":"Who chairs the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland, and for how long?","e":"In Scotland, the national Church is the Church of Scotland, which is a Presbyterian Church. It is governed by ministers and elders. The chairperson of the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland is the Moderator, who is appointed for one year only.","p":0,"o":[["The Moderator, appointed for one year only",1],["The Archbishop of Edinburgh, appointed for life",0],["The Moderator, appointed for five years",0],["The monarch, for the length of the reign",0]]},{"g":3240,"t":"In which TWO parts of the UK is there no established Church?","e":"There is no established Church in Wales or Northern Ireland. In England the established Church is the Church of England; in Scotland the national Church is the Church of Scotland.","p":0,"o":[["Wales",1],["Northern Ireland",1],["England",0],["Scotland",0]]},{"g":3241,"t":"How often are general elections held, at a minimum?","e":"MPs are elected at a General Election, which is held at least every five years. If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election, called a by-election, in his or her constituency.","p":2,"o":[["At least every five years",1],["At least every three years",0],["At least every seven years",0],["At least every four years",0]]},{"g":3242,"t":"What is the voting system used to elect MPs called?","e":"MPs are elected through a system called 'first past the post'. In each constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected. Elections to the European Parliament use a system of proportional representation.","p":2,"o":[["First past the post",1],["Proportional representation",0],["The single transferable vote",0],["A secret ballot of peers",0]]},{"g":3243,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe House of Commons has powers to overrule the House of Lords, but these are not used often.","e":"The House of Lords is normally more independent of the government than the House of Commons. The House of Commons has powers to overrule the House of Lords, but these are not used often.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3244,"t":"What happens if an MP dies or resigns?","e":"If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election, called a by-election, in his or her constituency.","p":2,"o":[["A by-election is held in that constituency",1],["A General Election is called immediately",0],["The seat stays empty until the next General Election",0],["The Prime Minister appoints a replacement",0]]},{"g":3245,"t":"As part of the citizenship ceremony, what do new citizens do?","e":"As part of the citizenship ceremony, new citizens pledge to uphold these values. The pledge includes giving loyalty to the United Kingdom, respecting its rights and freedoms, upholding its democratic values and observing its laws faithfully.","p":3,"o":[["They pledge to uphold British values",1],["They sit a second written examination",0],["They are given a British passport free of charge",0],["They must renounce all other citizenships in writing",0]]}]},
    {"n":503,"name":"Toughest Test 3","q":[{"g":3246,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Anne Boleyn was English. She and Henry had one daughter, Elizabeth. Anne was unpopular in the country and was accused of taking lovers. She was executed at the Tower of London. Catherine of Aragon was a Spanish princess.","p":1,"o":[["Anne Boleyn was executed at the Tower of London",1],["Catherine of Aragon was executed at the Tower of London",0]]},{"g":3247,"t":"Which of Henry VIII's wives gave him the son he wanted?","e":"Jane Seymour: Henry married Jane after Anne's execution. She gave Henry the son he wanted, Edward, but she died shortly after the birth.","p":1,"o":[["Jane Seymour",1],["Anne of Cleves",0],["Catherine Howard",0],["Catherine Parr",0]]},{"g":3248,"t":"Which TWO of Henry VIII's wives were executed?","e":"Anne Boleyn was accused of taking lovers and was executed at the Tower of London. Catherine Howard was a cousin of Anne Boleyn. She was also accused of taking lovers and executed.","p":1,"o":[["Anne Boleyn",1],["Catherine Howard",1],["Catherine Parr",0],["Jane Seymour",0]]},{"g":3249,"t":"Why did Henry VIII establish the Church of England?","e":"To divorce his first wife, Henry needed the approval of the Pope. When the Pope refused, Henry established the Church of England. In this new Church, the king, not the Pope, would have the power to appoint bishops.","p":1,"o":[["The Pope refused to approve his divorce",1],["Parliament ordered him to do so",0],["He wished to join the Reformation in Scotland",0],["He wanted to translate the Bible into English",0]]},{"g":3250,"t":"During whose reign was the Book of Common Prayer written?","e":"Henry VIII was succeeded by his son Edward VI, who was strongly Protestant. During his reign, the Book of Common Prayer was written to be used in the Church of England. Edward died at the age of 15.","p":1,"o":[["Edward VI",1],["Mary I",0],["Elizabeth I",0],["Henry VII",0]]},{"g":3251,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nMary I was a devout Catholic who persecuted Protestants and became known as 'Bloody Mary'.","e":"Mary was a devout Catholic and persecuted Protestants, and for this reason she became known as 'Bloody Mary'. Mary also died after a short reign and the next monarch was her half-sister Elizabeth.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3252,"t":"In 1588 the English defeated the Spanish Armada. Why had it been sent?","e":"Elizabeth became one of the most popular monarchs in English history, particularly after 1588, when the English defeated the Spanish Armada, a large fleet of ships, which had been sent by Spain to conquer England and restore Catholicism.","p":1,"o":[["To conquer England and restore Catholicism",1],["To capture the Golden Hind",0],["To support Mary, Queen of Scots in Scotland",0],["To colonise the eastern coast of America",0]]},{"g":3253,"t":"For how long did Elizabeth I keep Mary, Queen of Scots a prisoner?","e":"Mary was Elizabeth I's cousin and hoped that Elizabeth might help her, but Elizabeth suspected Mary of wanting to take over the English throne, and kept her a prisoner for 20 years. Mary was eventually executed.","p":1,"o":[["20 years",1],["11 years",0],["5 years",0],["30 years",0]]},{"g":3254,"t":"How old was Mary, Queen of Scots when she became queen of Scotland?","e":"The queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart, often now called 'Mary, Queen of Scots', was a Catholic. She was only a week old when her father died and she became queen. Much of her childhood was spent in France.","p":1,"o":[["She was only a week old",1],["She was 15",0],["She was 20",0],["She was a year old",0]]},{"g":3255,"t":"Which ship was one of the first to sail right around the world?","e":"Sir Francis Drake, one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada, was one of the founders of England's naval tradition. His ship, the Golden Hind, was one of the first to sail right around, or circumnavigate, the world.","p":1,"o":[["The Golden Hind",1],["HMS Victory",0],["The Mary Rose",0],["The Mayflower",0]]},{"g":3256,"t":"Which TWO lines below are from plays by William Shakespeare, according to the handbook?","e":"Lines from Shakespeare's plays and poems which are often still quoted include: Once more unto the breach from Henry V, To be or not to be from Hamlet, A rose by any other name from Romeo and Juliet, and All the world's a stage from As You Like It.","p":1,"o":[["To be or not to be",1],["Once more unto the breach",1],["I wander'd lonely as a cloud",0],["She walks in beauty, like the night",0]]},{"g":3257,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe Globe Theatre in London is the original theatre in which Shakespeare's plays were first performed.","e":"Many people regard Shakespeare as the greatest playwright of all time. The Globe Theatre in London is a modern copy of the theatres in which his plays were first performed.","p":1,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3258,"t":"Which national flower is associated with Wales?","e":"The countries that make up the UK all have flowers which are particularly associated with them: England the rose, Scotland the thistle, Wales the daffodil, and Northern Ireland the shamrock.","p":0,"o":[["The daffodil",1],["The thistle",0],["The shamrock",0],["The rose",0]]},{"g":3259,"t":"Which traditional food is a sheep's stomach stuffed with offal, suet, onions and oatmeal?","e":"Scotland: haggis, a sheep's stomach stuffed with offal, suet, onions and oatmeal. Northern Ireland has the Ulster fry and Wales has Welsh cakes.","p":0,"o":[["Haggis",1],["The Ulster fry",0],["Welsh cakes",0],["Yorkshire pudding",0]]},{"g":3260,"t":"Which TWO items are part of the traditional Ulster fry?","e":"Northern Ireland: Ulster fry, a fried meal with bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding, white pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread.","p":0,"o":[["Soda bread",1],["Potato bread",1],["Yorkshire pudding",0],["Welsh cakes",0]]},{"g":3261,"t":"In which year were films first shown publicly in the UK?","e":"Films were first shown publicly in the UK in 1896 and film screenings very quickly became popular. From the beginning, film makers became famous for clever special effects.","p":0,"o":[["1896",1],["1922",0],["1936",0],["1932",0]]},{"g":3262,"t":"For which character did Sir Charles Chaplin become famous in silent movies?","e":"From the early days of the cinema, British actors have worked in both the UK and USA. Sir Charles (Charlie) Chaplin became famous in silent movies for his tramp character and was one of many British actors to make a career in Hollywood.","p":0,"o":[["His tramp character",1],["Sherlock Holmes",0],["James Bond",0],["Wallace and Gromit",0]]},{"g":3263,"t":"How many Oscars has Nick Park won, and for what?","e":"Britain continues to be particularly strong in special effects and animation. One example is the work of Nick Park, who has won four Oscars for his animated films, including three for films featuring Wallace and Gromit.","p":0,"o":[["Four Oscars for his animated films",1],["Two Oscars for his documentaries",0],["Four Oscars for directing James Bond films",0],["One Oscar for special effects",0]]},{"g":3264,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Criminal law relates to crimes, which are usually investigated by the police or another authority such as a council, and which are punished by the courts. Civil law is used to settle disputes between individuals or groups.","p":2,"o":[["Civil law is used to settle disputes between individuals or groups",1],["Criminal law is used to settle disputes between individuals or groups",0]]},{"g":3265,"t":"Which TWO of these are given as examples of civil law?","e":"Examples of civil laws are housing law, including disputes between landlords and tenants; consumer rights, such as a dispute about faulty goods or services; employment law; and debt.","p":2,"o":[["Disputes between landlords and tenants",1],["A dispute about faulty goods or services",1],["Carrying a weapon for self-defence",0],["Selling tobacco to someone under 18",0]]},{"g":3266,"t":"What are the three duties of the police, according to the handbook?","e":"The job of the police in the UK is to protect life and property, prevent disturbances, also known as keeping the peace, and prevent and detect crime.","p":2,"o":[["Protect life and property, keep the peace, and prevent and detect crime",1],["Collect taxes, keep the peace, and run the courts",0],["Protect life and property, set sentences, and run prisons",0],["Investigate crime, pass laws, and appoint judges",0]]},{"g":3267,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nIt is a criminal offence to carry a weapon of any kind, even if it is for self-defence.","e":"Carrying a weapon: it is a criminal offence to carry a weapon of any kind, even if it is for self-defence. This includes a gun, a knife or anything that is made or adapted to cause injury.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3268,"t":"Who appoints the Chief Constable in England and Wales?","e":"Police and Crime Commissioners are directly elected individuals who are responsible for the delivery of an efficient and effective police force. PCCs set local police priorities and the local policing budget. They also appoint the Chief Constable.","p":2,"o":[["The Police and Crime Commissioner",1],["The Home Secretary",0],["The Prime Minister",0],["The local council",0]]},{"g":3269,"t":"Which of these are residents of the UK expected to do, according to the handbook?","e":"If you wish to be a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you should respect and obey the law, respect the rights of others including their right to their own opinions, treat others with fairness, look after yourself and your family, and look after the area in which you live and the environment.","p":3,"o":[["Look after the area in which they live and the environment",1],["Join a political party",0],["Attend a place of worship weekly",0],["Serve in the armed forces",0]]}]},
    {"n":504,"name":"Toughest Test 4","q":[{"g":3270,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"James I and his son Charles I both believed in the 'Divine Right of Kings': the idea that the king was directly appointed by God to rule. They thought that the king should be able to act without having to seek approval from Parliament.","p":1,"o":[["The Divine Right of Kings held that the king was directly appointed by God to rule",1],["The Divine Right of Kings held that Parliament appointed the king",0]]},{"g":3271,"t":"For how long did Charles I rule without Parliament before he was forced to recall it?","e":"When he could not get Parliament to agree with his religious and foreign policies, he tried to rule without Parliament at all. For 11 years, he found ways in which to raise money without Parliament's approval but eventually trouble in Scotland meant that he had to recall Parliament.","p":1,"o":[["11 years",1],["20 years",0],["5 years",0],["3 years",0]]},{"g":3272,"t":"What happened when Charles I entered the House of Commons to arrest five parliamentary leaders?","e":"Charles I entered the House of Commons and tried to arrest five parliamentary leaders, but they had been warned and were not there. No monarch has set foot in the Commons since.","p":1,"o":[["They had been warned and were not there",1],["They were arrested and executed",0],["They surrendered to the king",0],["Parliament voted to hand them over",0]]},{"g":3273,"t":"Which TWO names were given to the sides in the English civil war?","e":"Civil war between the king and Parliament could not now be avoided and began in 1642. The country split into those who supported the king, the Cavaliers, and those who supported Parliament, the Roundheads.","p":1,"o":[["The Cavaliers",1],["The Roundheads",1],["The Whigs",0],["The Tories",0]]},{"g":3274,"t":"At which TWO battles was the king's army defeated in the English civil war?","e":"The king's army was defeated at the Battles of Marston Moor and Naseby. By 1646, it was clear that Parliament had won the war.","p":1,"o":[["Marston Moor",1],["Naseby",1],["Dunbar",0],["Worcester",0]]},{"g":3275,"t":"What title was Oliver Cromwell given as leader of the new republic?","e":"After his campaign in Ireland and victory over Charles II at Worcester, Cromwell was recognised as the leader of the new republic. He was given the title of Lord Protector and ruled until his death in 1658.","p":1,"o":[["Lord Protector",1],["Lord Chancellor",0],["King of England",0],["Lord Lieutenant",0]]},{"g":3276,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nCharles II famously hid in an oak tree after his defeat at Worcester.","e":"Cromwell defeated this army in the Battles of Dunbar and Worcester. Charles II escaped from Worcester, famously hiding in an oak tree on one occasion, and eventually fled to Europe.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3277,"t":"From which country did Parliament invite Charles II to return in May 1660?","e":"In May 1660, Parliament invited Charles II to come back from exile in the Netherlands. He was crowned King Charles II of England, Wales, Scotland and Ireland.","p":1,"o":[["The Netherlands",1],["France",0],["Spain",0],["Scotland",0]]},{"g":3278,"t":"What does the Latin phrase 'habeas corpus' mean?","e":"The Habeas Corpus Act became law in 1679. Habeas corpus is Latin for 'you must present the person in court'. The Act guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully. Every prisoner has a right to a court hearing.","p":1,"o":[["You must present the person in court",1],["The king is subject to the law",0],["No taxation without representation",0],["Let the buyer beware",0]]},{"g":3279,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"During Charles II's reign, in 1665, there was a major outbreak of plague in London. The following year, a great fire destroyed much of the city, including many churches and St Paul's Cathedral.","p":1,"o":[["The plague in London came before the great fire",1],["The great fire of London came before the plague",0]]},{"g":3280,"t":"Who designed the new St Paul's Cathedral after the great fire?","e":"London was rebuilt with a new St Paul's, which was designed by a famous architect, Sir Christopher Wren. Samuel Pepys wrote about these events in a diary which was later published and is still read today.","p":1,"o":[["Sir Christopher Wren",1],["Inigo Jones",0],["Sir Edwin Lutyens",0],["Robert Adam",0]]},{"g":3281,"t":"What is the Royal Society, formed during the reign of Charles II?","e":"Charles II was interested in science. During his reign, the Royal Society was formed to promote 'natural knowledge'. This is the oldest surviving scientific society in the world. Among its early members were Sir Edmund Halley and Sir Isaac Newton.","p":1,"o":[["The oldest surviving scientific society in the world",1],["The first university in England",0],["The oldest theatre company in Britain",0],["An early anti-slavery group",0]]},{"g":3282,"t":"Which of these is NOT a responsibility of an MP, as listed in the handbook?","e":"MPs represent everyone in their constituency, help to create new laws, scrutinise and comment on what the government is doing, and debate important national issues.","p":2,"o":[["Appointing judges to the courts",1],["Representing everyone in their constituency",0],["Helping to create new laws",0],["Debating important national issues",0]]},{"g":3283,"t":"Until 1958, which TWO groups sat in the House of Lords alongside hereditary peers?","e":"Until 1958, all peers were 'hereditary', which means they inherited their title, or senior judges, or bishops of the Church of England.","p":2,"o":[["Senior judges",1],["Bishops of the Church of England",1],["Elected councillors",0],["Members of the European Parliament",0]]},{"g":3284,"t":"What changed for hereditary peers in 1999?","e":"Since 1999, hereditary peers have lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords. They now elect a few of their number to represent them in the House of Lords.","p":2,"o":[["They lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords",1],["They were all removed from the House of Lords permanently",0],["They were given the right to stand for the House of Commons",0],["They gained the power to overrule the Commons",0]]},{"g":3285,"t":"Who formally appoints life peers?","e":"Since 1958, the Prime Minister has had the power to nominate peers just for their own lifetime. These are called life peers. Life peers are appointed by the monarch on the advice of the Prime Minister.","p":2,"o":[["The monarch, on the advice of the Prime Minister",1],["The Speaker of the House of Commons",0],["The House of Commons by a vote",0],["The Appointments Commission alone",0]]},{"g":3286,"t":"How many members are there in the Scottish Parliament?","e":"The Scottish Parliament was formed in 1999. It sits in Edinburgh. There are 129 members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs), elected by a form of proportional representation.","p":2,"o":[["129",1],["60",0],["108",0],["33",0]]},{"g":3287,"t":"How many Assembly Members does the National Assembly for Wales have, and how often are elections held?","e":"The Welsh government and National Assembly for Wales are based in Cardiff. The National Assembly has 60 Assembly members (AMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation.","p":2,"o":[["60 AMs, elected every four years",1],["129 AMs, elected every four years",0],["60 AMs, elected every five years",0],["90 AMs, elected every four years",0]]},{"g":3288,"t":"Which TWO matters remain under central UK government control rather than being devolved?","e":"Policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security all remain under central UK government control. However, many other public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations.","p":2,"o":[["Defence",1],["Foreign affairs",1],["Education",0],["Health",0]]},{"g":3289,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nSince 2011 the National Assembly for Wales has been able to pass laws on its 20 devolved topics without the agreement of the UK Parliament.","e":"The Assembly has the power to make laws for Wales in 20 areas, including education and training, health and social services, economic development, and housing. Since 2011, the National Assembly for Wales has been able to pass laws on these topics without the agreement of the UK Parliament.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3290,"t":"In which building do members of the Scottish Parliament meet?","e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh.","p":2,"o":[["Holyrood, in Edinburgh",1],["Stormont, in Belfast",0],["The Senedd, in Cardiff Bay",0],["The Palace of Westminster",0]]},{"g":3291,"t":"Where are the official reports of proceedings in Parliament published?","e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard. Written reports can be found in large libraries and at www.parliament.uk.","p":2,"o":[["In Hansard",1],["In The Phone Book",0],["In Yellow Pages",0],["In the Domesday Book",0]]},{"g":3292,"t":"What does it mean to say the UK has a free press?","e":"The UK has a free press. This means that what is written in newspapers is free from government control. Some newspaper owners and editors hold strong political opinions and run campaigns to try to influence government policy and public opinion.","p":2,"o":[["What is written in newspapers is free from government control",1],["Newspapers are given away without charge",0],["Newspapers may not express political opinions",0],["Newspapers are funded by the television licence",0]]},{"g":3293,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"By law, radio and television coverage of the political parties must be balanced and so equal time has to be given to rival viewpoints. Newspapers, by contrast, are free of government control and their owners may run campaigns.","p":2,"o":[["Radio and television coverage of the political parties must be balanced by law",1],["Newspaper coverage of the political parties must be balanced by law",0]]}]},
    {"n":505,"name":"Toughest Test 5","q":[{"g":3294,"t":"The Bill of Rights of 1689 confirmed which of these?","e":"The Bill of Rights, 1689, confirmed the rights of Parliament and the limits of the king's power. Parliament took control of who could be monarch and declared that the king or queen must be a Protestant. Every year the monarch had to ask Parliament to renew funding for the army and the navy.","p":1,"o":[["That the king or queen must be a Protestant",1],["That the monarch could raise taxes without Parliament",0],["That the monarch could appoint judges freely",0],["That Parliament would meet only when the king summoned it",0]]},{"g":3295,"t":"After the Bill of Rights, how often did a new Parliament have to be elected?","e":"A new Parliament had to be elected at least every three years. Later this became seven years and now it is five years.","p":1,"o":[["At least every three years",1],["At least every five years",0],["At least every seven years",0],["At least every year",0]]},{"g":3296,"t":"Which TWO groups were the main political groupings in Parliament after the Glorious Revolution?","e":"There were two main groups in Parliament, known as the Whigs and the Tories. The modern Conservative Party is still sometimes referred to as the Tories. This was the beginning of party politics.","p":1,"o":[["The Whigs",1],["The Tories",1],["The Cavaliers",0],["The Chartists",0]]},{"g":3297,"t":"What happened from 1695 that was important for the development of a free press?","e":"This was also an important time for the development of a free press, newspapers and other publications which are not controlled by the government. From 1695, newspapers were allowed to operate without a government licence.","p":1,"o":[["Newspapers were allowed to operate without a government licence",1],["The first newspaper in England was printed",0],["The government began to fund newspapers",0],["Newspapers were required to be balanced by law",0]]},{"g":3298,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Some constituencies were controlled by a single wealthy family. These were called 'pocket boroughs'. Other constituencies had hardly any voters and were called 'rotten boroughs'.","p":1,"o":[["A constituency controlled by a single wealthy family was called a pocket borough",1],["A constituency controlled by a single wealthy family was called a rotten borough",0]]},{"g":3299,"t":"Which refugees came to Britain from France between 1680 and 1720?","e":"Between 1680 and 1720 many refugees called Huguenots came from France. They were Protestants and had been persecuted for their religion. Many were educated and skilled and worked as scientists, in banking, or in weaving or other crafts.","p":1,"o":[["The Huguenots",1],["The Jacobites",0],["The Boers",0],["The Puritans",0]]},{"g":3300,"t":"The Act of Union of 1707 created which kingdom?","e":"The Act of Union, known as the Treaty of Union in Scotland, was agreed in 1707, creating the Kingdom of Great Britain. Although Scotland was no longer an independent country, it kept its own legal and education systems and Presbyterian Church.","p":1,"o":[["The Kingdom of Great Britain",1],["The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland",0],["The Commonwealth",0],["The Kingdom of England and Wales",0]]},{"g":3301,"t":"Which TWO things did Scotland keep after the Act of Union of 1707?","e":"Although Scotland was no longer an independent country, it kept its own legal and education systems and Presbyterian Church.","p":1,"o":[["Its own legal system",1],["Its own education system",1],["Its own parliament",0],["Its own currency",0]]},{"g":3302,"t":"Why did Parliament choose George I to be king in 1714?","e":"When Queen Anne died in 1714, Parliament chose a German, George I, to be the next king, because he was Anne's nearest Protestant relative. George I did not speak very good English and this increased his need to rely on his ministers.","p":1,"o":[["He was Anne's nearest Protestant relative",1],["He was Anne's eldest son",0],["He had won a war against France",0],["He was elected by the people",0]]},{"g":3303,"t":"Who was the first man to be called Prime Minister, and in which years did he serve?","e":"The most important minister in Parliament became known as the Prime Minister. The first man to be called this was Sir Robert Walpole, who was Prime Minister from 1721 to 1742.","p":1,"o":[["Sir Robert Walpole, from 1721 to 1742",1],["The Duke of Wellington, from 1721 to 1742",0],["Sir Robert Walpole, from 1746 to 1783",0],["William Wilberforce, from 1721 to 1742",0]]},{"g":3304,"t":"At which battle was Bonnie Prince Charlie defeated in 1746?","e":"In 1745 there was another attempt to put a Stuart king back on the throne. Charles Edward Stuart, Bonnie Prince Charlie, the grandson of James II, landed in Scotland. He was defeated by George II's army at the Battle of Culloden in 1746.","p":1,"o":[["Culloden",1],["Killiecrankie",0],["Bannockburn",0],["The Boyne",0]]},{"g":3305,"t":"What were the 'Highland Clearances'?","e":"Many Scottish landlords destroyed individual small farms, known as 'crofts', to make space for large flocks of sheep and cattle. Evictions became very common in the early 19th century. Many Scottish people left for North America at this time.","p":1,"o":[["Small farms were destroyed to make space for sheep and cattle",1],["Highland forests were cleared for shipbuilding",0],["Clan chieftains were exiled to France",0],["Castles in the Highlands were demolished",0]]},{"g":3306,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nRobert Burns wrote only in standard English.","e":"Known in Scotland as 'The Bard', Robert Burns was a Scottish poet. He wrote in the Scots language, English with some Scottish words, and standard English. His best-known work is probably the song Auld Lang Syne.","p":1,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3307,"t":"Which song by Robert Burns is sung when people celebrate the New Year?","e":"Burns' best-known work is probably the song Auld Lang Syne, which is sung by people in the UK and other countries when they are celebrating the New Year, or Hogmanay as it is called in Scotland.","p":1,"o":[["Auld Lang Syne",1],["Land of Hope and Glory",0],["I Vow to Thee, My Country",0],["God Save the King",0]]},{"g":3308,"t":"Which TWO Scottish thinkers of the Enlightenment are named in the handbook?","e":"Many of the great thinkers of the Enlightenment were Scottish. Adam Smith developed ideas about economics which are still referred to today. David Hume's ideas about human nature continue to influence philosophers.","p":1,"o":[["Adam Smith",1],["David Hume",1],["Isaac Newton",0],["Richard Arkwright",0]]},{"g":3309,"t":"What is one of the most important principles of the Enlightenment, according to the handbook?","e":"One of the most important principles of the Enlightenment was that everyone should have the right to their own political and religious beliefs and that the state should not try to dictate to them. This continues to be an important principle in the UK today.","p":1,"o":[["Everyone should have the right to their own political and religious beliefs",1],["The state should control religious worship",0],["Only property owners should have political rights",0],["Science should be kept separate from philosophy",0]]},{"g":3310,"t":"How long does giving blood take, according to the handbook?","e":"Donated blood is used by hospitals to help people with a wide range of injuries and illnesses. Giving blood only takes about an hour to do.","p":2,"o":[["About an hour",1],["About five minutes",0],["About a whole day",0],["About three hours",0]]},{"g":3311,"t":"Which organ can living people donate?","e":"Many people in the UK are waiting for organ transplants. If you register to be an organ donor, it can make it easier for your family to decide whether to donate your organs when you die. Living people can also donate a kidney.","p":2,"o":[["A kidney",1],["A heart",0],["A liver",0],["A lung",0]]},{"g":3312,"t":"At what age can a person become a school governor, and is there an upper age limit?","e":"School governors, or members of the school board in Scotland, must be aged 18 or over at the date of their election or appointment. There is no upper age limit.","p":2,"o":[["18 or over, with no upper age limit",1],["18 or over, up to the age of 70",0],["21 or over, with no upper age limit",0],["16 or over, up to the age of 70",0]]},{"g":3313,"t":"Which TWO are key roles of school governors, according to the handbook?","e":"Governors and school boards have three key roles: setting the strategic direction of the school, ensuring accountability, and monitoring and evaluating school performance.","p":2,"o":[["Setting the strategic direction of the school",1],["Monitoring and evaluating school performance",1],["Teaching lessons when staff are absent",0],["Setting the national curriculum",0]]},{"g":3314,"t":"What is 'canvassing'?","e":"Political parties are particularly busy at election times. Members work hard to persuade people to vote for their candidates, for instance by handing out leaflets in the street or by knocking on people's doors and asking for their support. This is called 'canvassing'.","p":2,"o":[["Asking people for their support, for example by knocking on doors",1],["Counting the votes after an election",0],["Registering to vote at your local council",0],["Standing as a candidate for Parliament",0]]},{"g":3315,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nYou have to tell a canvasser how you intend to vote.","e":"Members work hard to persuade people to vote for their candidates. This is called 'canvassing'. You don't have to tell a canvasser how you intend to vote if you don't want to.","p":2,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3316,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"It is important to recycle as much of your waste as you can. Using recycled materials to make new products uses less energy. It also means that less rubbish is created, so the amount being put into landfill is reduced.","p":2,"o":[["Recycling uses less energy and puts less rubbish into landfill",1],["Recycling uses more energy and puts more rubbish into landfill",0]]},{"g":3317,"t":"Which TWO of these does the handbook suggest as ways to look after the environment?","e":"A good way to support your local community is to shop for products locally where you can. Walking and using public transport to get around when you can is also a good way to protect the environment. It means that you create less pollution than when you use a car.","p":2,"o":[["Shop locally where you can",1],["Walk or use public transport",1],["Use a car for every journey",0],["Put all rubbish into landfill",0]]}]},
    {"n":506,"name":"Toughest Test 6","q":[{"g":3318,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Gustav Holst's work includes The Planets, a suite of pieces themed around the planets of the solar system. Sir Edward Elgar's best-known work is probably the Pomp and Circumstance Marches; March No 1 is known as Land of Hope and Glory.","p":0,"o":[["Gustav Holst wrote The Planets",1],["Sir Edward Elgar wrote The Planets",0]]},{"g":3319,"t":"Which hymn did Gustav Holst adapt from part of The Planets suite?","e":"He adapted Jupiter, part of the Planets suite, as the tune for I vow to thee my country, a popular hymn in British churches.","p":0,"o":[["I vow to thee my country",1],["Land of Hope and Glory",0],["Auld Lang Syne",0],["God Save the King",0]]},{"g":3320,"t":"Where was Henry Purcell the organist?","e":"Henry Purcell (1659-95) was the organist at Westminster Abbey. He wrote church music, operas and other pieces, and developed a British style distinct from that elsewhere in Europe.","p":0,"o":[["Westminster Abbey",1],["St Paul's Cathedral",0],["The Royal Albert Hall",0],["York Minster",0]]},{"g":3321,"t":"George Frederick Handel wrote the Water Music for which king?","e":"The German-born composer George Frederick Handel spent many years in the UK and became a British citizen in 1727. He wrote the Water Music for King George I and Music for the Royal Fireworks for his son, George II.","p":0,"o":[["King George I",1],["King George II",0],["King Charles II",0],["King James I",0]]},{"g":3322,"t":"Which TWO operas are among Benjamin Britten's best-known works?","e":"Benjamin Britten (1913-76) is best known for his operas, which include Peter Grimes and Billy Budd. He also wrote A Young Person's Guide to the Orchestra, which is based on a piece of music by Purcell.","p":0,"o":[["Peter Grimes",1],["Billy Budd",1],["The Mikado",0],["HMS Pinafore",0]]},{"g":3323,"t":"Which festival did Benjamin Britten found?","e":"He founded the Aldeburgh festival in Suffolk, which continues to be a popular music event of international importance.","p":0,"o":[["The Aldeburgh festival, in Suffolk",1],["The Edinburgh Festival",0],["The National Eisteddfod of Wales",0],["The Glastonbury Festival",0]]},{"g":3324,"t":"How long is the Proms season, and who has organised it?","e":"The Proms is an eight-week summer season of orchestral classical music that takes place in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London. It has been organised by the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC) since 1927.","p":0,"o":[["Eight weeks, organised by the BBC since 1927",1],["Four weeks, organised by the BBC since 1927",0],["Eight weeks, organised by the BBC since 1922",0],["Two weeks, organised by the Royal Albert Hall",0]]},{"g":3325,"t":"What is awarded each September for the best album from the UK and Ireland?","e":"The Mercury Music Prize is awarded each September for the best album from the UK and Ireland. The Brit Awards is an annual event that gives awards in a range of categories, such as best British group and best British solo artist.","p":0,"o":[["The Mercury Music Prize",1],["The Brit Awards",0],["The Turner Prize",0],["The Laurence Olivier Awards",0]]},{"g":3326,"t":"Which play has been running in the West End since 1952?","e":"London's West End, also known as 'Theatreland', is particularly well known. The Mousetrap, a murder-mystery play by Dame Agatha Christie, has been running in the West End since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history.","p":0,"o":[["The Mousetrap",1],["The Phantom of the Opera",0],["Cats",0],["Evita",0]]},{"g":3327,"t":"Which TWO comic operas were written by Gilbert and Sullivan?","e":"In the 19th century, Gilbert and Sullivan wrote comic operas, often making fun of popular culture and politics. These operas include HMS Pinafore, The Pirates of Penzance and The Mikado.","p":0,"o":[["The Pirates of Penzance",1],["The Mikado",1],["Jesus Christ Superstar",0],["Peter Grimes",0]]},{"g":3328,"t":"In a traditional British pantomime, who is the Dame?","e":"One British tradition is the pantomime. Many theatres produce a pantomime at Christmas time. One of the traditional characters is the Dame, a woman played by a man. There is often also a pantomime horse or cow played by two actors.","p":0,"o":[["A woman played by a man",1],["A man played by a woman",0],["The villain of the story",0],["A horse played by two actors",0]]},{"g":3329,"t":"Which part of the Edinburgh Festival is the biggest and best known?","e":"The Edinburgh Festival takes place in Edinburgh, Scotland, every summer. It is a series of different arts and cultural festivals, with the biggest and most well-known being the Edinburgh Festival Fringe, 'the Fringe'.","p":0,"o":[["The Edinburgh Festival Fringe",1],["The Last Night of the Proms",0],["The National Eisteddfod",0],["The Turner Prize exhibition",0]]},{"g":3330,"t":"After which British actor are the Laurence Olivier Awards named?","e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London. The awards are named after the British actor Sir Laurence Olivier, later Lord Olivier, who was best known for his roles in various Shakespeare plays.","p":0,"o":[["An actor best known for his roles in Shakespeare plays",1],["A composer of coronation marches",0],["A director of silent films",0],["A designer of Art Deco ceramics",0]]},{"g":3331,"t":"In which year was the Turner Prize established, and where are the shortlisted works shown?","e":"The Turner Prize was established in 1984 and celebrates contemporary art. It was named after Joseph Turner. Four works are shortlisted every year and shown at Tate Britain before the winner is announced.","p":0,"o":[["1984, and the works are shown at Tate Britain",1],["1968, and the works are shown at Tate Britain",0],["1984, and the works are shown at the National Gallery",0],["1927, and the works are shown at Tate Modern",0]]},{"g":3332,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"John Constable (1776-1837) was a landscape painter most famous for his works of Dedham Vale on the Suffolk-Essex border in the east of England. Joseph Turner was an influential landscape painter in a modern style.","p":0,"o":[["John Constable is most famous for his works of Dedham Vale",1],["Joseph Turner is most famous for his works of Dedham Vale",0]]},{"g":3333,"t":"Which TWO artists were members of the Pre-Raphaelites?","e":"The Pre-Raphaelites were an important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century. They painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. The group included Holman Hunt, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and Sir John Millais.","p":0,"o":[["Holman Hunt",1],["Sir John Millais",1],["Henry Moore",0],["David Hockney",0]]},{"g":3334,"t":"For what is Henry Moore best known?","e":"Henry Moore (1898-1986) was an English sculptor and artist. He is best known for his large bronze abstract sculptures.","p":0,"o":[["Large bronze abstract sculptures",1],["Engravings and stained glass",0],["Portraits of the Royal Family",0],["Pop art of the 1960s",0]]},{"g":3335,"t":"Which artist was a Welsh engraver and stained glass maker?","e":"John Petts (1914-91) was a Welsh artist, best known for his engravings and stained glass. Sir John Lavery was a Northern Irish portrait painter.","p":0,"o":[["John Petts",1],["Sir John Lavery",0],["David Allan",0],["Lucian Freud",0]]},{"g":3336,"t":"Which building in the Tower of London is an example of a Norman castle keep?","e":"The White Tower in the Tower of London is an example of a Norman castle keep, built on the orders of William the Conqueror.","p":0,"o":[["The White Tower",1],["The Banqueting House",0],["The Queen's House",0],["Elizabeth Tower",0]]},{"g":3337,"t":"Which TWO buildings did Inigo Jones design, taking inspiration from classical architecture?","e":"In the 17th century, Inigo Jones took inspiration from classical architecture to design the Queen's House at Greenwich and the Banqueting House in Whitehall in London.","p":0,"o":[["The Queen's House at Greenwich",1],["The Banqueting House in Whitehall",1],["The new St Paul's Cathedral",0],["St Pancras Station",0]]},{"g":3338,"t":"Who designed New Delhi to be the seat of government in India, and also the Cenotaph?","e":"In the 20th century, Sir Edwin Lutyens had an influence throughout the British Empire. He designed New Delhi to be the seat of government in India. After the First World War, he was responsible for many war memorials, including the Cenotaph in Whitehall.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Edwin Lutyens",1],["Robert Adam",0],["Sir Christopher Wren",0],["Sir Norman Foster",0]]},{"g":3339,"t":"Which garden designer often said that a place had 'capabilities'?","e":"In the 18th century, Lancelot 'Capability' Brown designed the grounds around country houses so that the landscape appeared to be natural, with grass, trees and lakes. He often said that a place had 'capabilities'.","p":0,"o":[["Lancelot 'Capability' Brown",1],["Gertrude Jekyll",0],["Sir Edwin Lutyens",0],["Robert Adam",0]]},{"g":3340,"t":"Which annual show showcases garden design from Britain and around the world?","e":"Gardens continue to be an important part of homes in the UK. The annual Chelsea Flower Show showcases garden design from Britain and around the world.","p":0,"o":[["The Chelsea Flower Show",1],["The Great Exhibition",0],["The National Eisteddfod",0],["The Edinburgh Festival",0]]},{"g":3341,"t":"Which of these designers is associated with Art Deco ceramics?","e":"Britain has produced many great designers, from Thomas Chippendale, who designed furniture in the 18th century, to Clarice Cliff, who designed Art Deco ceramics, to Sir Terence Conran, a 20th-century interior designer.","p":0,"o":[["Clarice Cliff",1],["Thomas Chippendale",0],["Sir Terence Conran",0],["Mary Quant",0]]}]},
    {"n":507,"name":"Toughest Test 7","q":[{"g":3342,"t":"On how many occasions has the UK hosted the Olympic Games, and in which years?","e":"The UK has hosted the Olympic Games on three occasions: 1908, 1948 and 2012. The main Olympic site for the 2012 Games was in Stratford, East London.","p":0,"o":[["Three: 1908, 1948 and 2012",1],["Three: 1908, 1936 and 2012",0],["Two: 1948 and 2012",0],["Four: 1908, 1948, 1966 and 2012",0]]},{"g":3343,"t":"Where did the British team finish in the medal table at the 2012 Olympic Games?","e":"The main Olympic site for the 2012 Games was in Stratford, East London. The British team was very successful, across a wide range of Olympic sports, finishing third in the medal table.","p":0,"o":[["Third",1],["First",0],["Second",0],["Fifth",0]]},{"g":3344,"t":"Where did the Paralympic Games have their origin?","e":"The Paralympics have their origin in the work of Dr Sir Ludwig Guttman, a German refugee, at the Stoke Mandeville hospital in Buckinghamshire. Dr Guttman developed new methods of treatment for people with spinal injuries and encouraged patients to take part in exercise and sport.","p":0,"o":[["In the work of Dr Sir Ludwig Guttman at Stoke Mandeville hospital",1],["In the 1908 London Olympic Games",0],["In the work of Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson",0],["At the Millennium Stadium in Cardiff",0]]},{"g":3345,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Sir Roger Bannister was the first man in the world to run a mile in under four minutes, in 1954. Sir Jackie Stewart is a Scottish former racing driver who won the Formula 1 world championship three times.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Roger Bannister was the first man to run a mile in under four minutes",1],["Sir Jackie Stewart was the first man to run a mile in under four minutes",0]]},{"g":3346,"t":"Who captained the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966?","e":"Bobby Moore (1941-93) captained the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966. England's only international tournament victory was at the World Cup of 1966, hosted in the UK.","p":0,"o":[["Bobby Moore",1],["Sir Ian Botham",0],["Sir Steve Redgrave",0],["Sir Chris Hoy",0]]},{"g":3347,"t":"In which year did Jayne Torvill and Christopher Dean win Olympic gold medals for ice dancing?","e":"Jayne Torvill (1957-) and Christopher Dean (1958-) won gold medals for ice dancing at the Olympic Games in 1984 and in four consecutive world championships.","p":0,"o":[["1984",1],["1966",0],["2004",0],["1954",0]]},{"g":3348,"t":"For what achievement is Sir Steve Redgrave known?","e":"Sir Steve Redgrave (1962-) won gold medals in rowing in five consecutive Olympic Games and is one of Britain's greatest Olympians.","p":0,"o":[["Winning rowing gold medals in five consecutive Olympic Games",1],["Winning six Olympic gold medals in cycling",0],["Winning the Formula 1 world championship three times",0],["Winning 16 Paralympic medals",0]]},{"g":3349,"t":"Which TWO of these people won the London Marathon six times, according to the handbook?","e":"Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson won 16 Paralympic medals, including 11 gold medals, and won the London Marathon six times. David Weir is a Paralympian who has won six gold medals over two Paralympic Games and has also won the London Marathon six times.","p":0,"o":[["Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson",1],["David Weir",1],["Dame Kelly Holmes",0],["Mo Farah",0]]},{"g":3350,"t":"What did Dame Ellen MacArthur achieve in 2004?","e":"Dame Ellen MacArthur (1976-) is a yachtswoman and in 2004 became the fastest person to sail around the world singlehanded.","p":0,"o":[["She became the fastest person to sail around the world singlehanded",1],["She won two Olympic gold medals for running",0],["She became the first Briton to win the Tour de France",0],["She won Olympic gold in the heptathlon",0]]},{"g":3351,"t":"In 2012 Bradley Wiggins became the first Briton to win which event?","e":"Bradley Wiggins (1980-) is a cyclist. In 2012, he became the first Briton to win the Tour de France. He has won seven Olympic medals.","p":0,"o":[["The Tour de France",1],["The Formula 1 world championship",0],["The Open Championship",0],["The Grand National",0]]},{"g":3352,"t":"Which TWO distances did Mo Farah win gold medals for at the 2012 Olympics?","e":"Mo Farah (1983-) is a British distance runner, born in Somalia. He won gold medals in the 2012 Olympics for the 5,000 and 10,000 metres and is the first Briton to win the Olympic gold medal in the 10,000 metres.","p":0,"o":[["5,000 metres",1],["10,000 metres",1],["1,500 metres",0],["800 metres",0]]},{"g":3353,"t":"How many track and field events make up the heptathlon, which Jessica Ennis won in 2012?","e":"Jessica Ennis (1986-) is an athlete. She won the 2012 Olympic gold medal in the heptathlon, which includes seven different track and field events.","p":0,"o":[["Seven",1],["Five",0],["Ten",0],["Three",0]]},{"g":3354,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Andy Murray (1987-) is a Scottish tennis player who in 2012 won the men's singles in the US Open. He is the first British man to win a singles title in a Grand Slam tournament since 1936.","p":0,"o":[["Andy Murray was the first British man to win a Grand Slam singles title since 1936",1],["Andy Murray was the first British man to win a Grand Slam singles title since 1966",0]]},{"g":3355,"t":"What is the most famous cricket competition, and between which countries is it played?","e":"The most famous competition is the Ashes, which is a series of Test matches played between England and Australia.","p":0,"o":[["The Ashes, between England and Australia",1],["The Ashes, between England and India",0],["The Six Nations, between England and Australia",0],["The Super League, between England and Australia",0]]},{"g":3356,"t":"Which of these is NOT one of the six countries in the Six Nations Championship?","e":"The most famous rugby union competition is the Six Nations Championship between England, Ireland, Scotland, Wales, France and Italy. The Super League is the most well-known rugby league competition.","p":0,"o":[["Spain",1],["France",0],["Italy",0],["Ireland",0]]},{"g":3357,"t":"Which TWO horse-racing events are named in the handbook alongside Royal Ascot?","e":"Famous horse-racing events include Royal Ascot, a five-day race meeting in Berkshire attended by members of the Royal Family; the Grand National at Aintree near Liverpool; and the Scottish Grand National at Ayr.","p":0,"o":[["The Grand National at Aintree",1],["The Scottish Grand National at Ayr",1],["The Open Championship",0],["The Wimbledon Championships",0]]},{"g":3358,"t":"To which century can the modern game of golf be traced back, and to which country?","e":"The modern game of golf can be traced back to 15th century Scotland. It is a popular sport played socially as well as professionally. St Andrews in Scotland is known as the home of golf.","p":0,"o":[["15th century Scotland",1],["19th century England",0],["15th century England",0],["18th century Scotland",0]]},{"g":3359,"t":"What is distinctive about The Open Championship in golf?","e":"The Open Championship is the only 'Major' tournament held outside the United States. It is hosted by a different golf course every year.","p":0,"o":[["It is the only 'Major' tournament held outside the United States",1],["It is the oldest tournament in the world",0],["It is the only Major played on grass",0],["It is always held at St Andrews",0]]},{"g":3360,"t":"Where and when was the first tennis club founded?","e":"Modern tennis evolved in England in the late 19th century. The first tennis club was founded in Leamington Spa in 1872.","p":0,"o":[["Leamington Spa, in 1872",1],["Wimbledon, in 1872",0],["Leamington Spa, in 1902",0],["St Andrews, in 1872",0]]},{"g":3361,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe Wimbledon Championships is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only Grand Slam event played on grass.","e":"The most famous tournament hosted in Britain is The Wimbledon Championships, which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club. It is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only 'Grand Slam' event played on grass.","p":0,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3362,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"A British sailor, Sir Francis Chichester, was the first person to sail single-handed around the world, in 1966/67. Two years later, Sir Robin Knox-Johnston became the first person to do this without stopping.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Robin Knox-Johnston was the first to sail around the world single-handed without stopping",1],["Sir Francis Chichester was the first to sail around the world single-handed without stopping",0]]},{"g":3363,"t":"In which year did motor-car racing start in the UK?","e":"There is a long history of motor sport in the UK, for both cars and motor cycles. Motor-car racing in the UK started in 1902. The UK continues to be a world leader in the development and manufacture of motor-sport technology.","p":0,"o":[["1902",1],["1896",0],["1922",0],["1872",0]]},{"g":3364,"t":"How many ski centres are there in Scotland, according to the handbook?","e":"Skiing is increasingly popular in the UK. Many people go abroad to ski and there are also dry ski slopes throughout the UK. There are five ski centres in Scotland, as well as Europe's longest dry ski slope near Edinburgh.","p":0,"o":[["Five",1],["Three",0],["Fifteen",0],["Ten",0]]},{"g":3365,"t":"Which TWO stadiums are named in the handbook as major venues for sporting events?","e":"Many sporting events take place at major stadiums such as Wembley Stadium in London and the Millennium Stadium in Cardiff.","p":0,"o":[["Wembley Stadium",1],["The Millennium Stadium",1],["The O2 in Greenwich",0],["The Scottish Exhibition and Conference Centre",0]]}]},
    {"n":508,"name":"Toughest Test 8","q":[{"g":3366,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In 1807, it became illegal to trade slaves in British ships or from British ports, and in 1833 the Emancipation Act abolished slavery throughout the British Empire.","p":1,"o":[["The Emancipation Act of 1833 abolished slavery throughout the British Empire",1],["The Emancipation Act of 1807 abolished slavery throughout the British Empire",0]]},{"g":3367,"t":"Who set up the first formal anti-slavery groups in Britain?","e":"There were people in Britain who opposed the slave trade. The first formal anti-slavery groups were set up by the Quakers in the late 1700s, and they petitioned Parliament to ban the practice.","p":1,"o":[["The Quakers",1],["The Puritans",0],["The Huguenots",0],["The Chartists",0]]},{"g":3368,"t":"Who was William Wilberforce?","e":"William Wilberforce, an evangelical Christian and a member of Parliament, played an important part in changing the law. Along with other abolitionists, he succeeded in turning public opinion against the slave trade.","p":1,"o":[["An evangelical Christian and MP who helped turn opinion against the slave trade",1],["A Quaker who founded the first anti-slavery group",0],["The admiral who commanded the fleet at Trafalgar",0],["The first Prime Minister of Great Britain",0]]},{"g":3369,"t":"What did the American colonists mean by 'no taxation without representation'?","e":"The British government wanted to tax the colonies. The colonists saw this as an attack on their freedom and said there should be 'no taxation without representation' in the British Parliament.","p":1,"o":[["They should not be taxed without representation in the British Parliament",1],["They should not pay taxes to their own colonial assemblies",0],["Only landowners should be taxed",0],["Taxes should be collected by the Royal Navy",0]]},{"g":3370,"t":"In which year did Britain recognise the independence of the American colonies?","e":"In 1776, 13 American colonies declared their independence, stating that people had a right to establish their own governments. The colonists eventually defeated the British army and Britain recognised the colonies' independence in 1783.","p":1,"o":[["1783",1],["1776",0],["1789",0],["1801",0]]},{"g":3371,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Britain's navy fought against combined French and Spanish fleets, winning the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805. Admiral Nelson was in charge of the British fleet at Trafalgar and was killed in the battle. In 1815, the French Wars ended with the defeat of Napoleon by the Duke of Wellington at the Battle of Waterloo.","p":1,"o":[["Nelson commanded at Trafalgar and Wellington at Waterloo",1],["Wellington commanded at Trafalgar and Nelson at Waterloo",0]]},{"g":3372,"t":"Where can Admiral Nelson's ship, HMS Victory, be visited?","e":"Nelson's Column in Trafalgar Square, London, is a monument to him. His ship, HMS Victory, can be visited in Portsmouth.","p":1,"o":[["Portsmouth",1],["Greenwich",0],["Liverpool",0],["Plymouth",0]]},{"g":3373,"t":"By what nickname was the Duke of Wellington known?","e":"In 1815, the French Wars ended with the defeat of the Emperor Napoleon by the Duke of Wellington at the Battle of Waterloo. Wellington was known as the Iron Duke and later became Prime Minister.","p":1,"o":[["The Iron Duke",1],["The Bard",0],["The Lord Protector",0],["Bonnie Prince Charlie",0]]},{"g":3374,"t":"Which TWO crosses, besides the cross of St George, make up the Union Flag?","e":"The Union Flag consists of three crosses: the cross of St George, patron saint of England, a red cross on a white ground; the cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland, a diagonal white cross on a blue ground; and the cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland, a diagonal red cross on a white ground.","p":1,"o":[["The cross of St Andrew",1],["The cross of St Patrick",1],["The cross of St David",0],["The Welsh dragon",0]]},{"g":3375,"t":"Why does the Welsh dragon not appear on the Union Flag?","e":"The Welsh dragon does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already united with England.","p":1,"o":[["Wales was already united with England when the first Union Flag was created",1],["Wales had its own separate parliament at the time",0],["The dragon was thought to be unlucky",0],["Wales did not have a flag until the 19th century",0]]},{"g":3376,"t":"How old was Queen Victoria when she became queen, and for roughly how long did she reign?","e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18. She reigned until 1901, almost 64 years. Her reign is known as the Victorian Age.","p":1,"o":[["18, and she reigned almost 64 years",1],["21, and she reigned almost 64 years",0],["18, and she reigned almost 50 years",0],["25, and she reigned almost 70 years",0]]},{"g":3377,"t":"What was the estimated population of the British Empire at its height in the Victorian period?","e":"During the Victorian period, the British Empire grew to cover all of India, Australia and large parts of Africa. It became the largest empire the world has ever seen, with an estimated population of more than 400 million people.","p":1,"o":[["More than 400 million",1],["More than 40 million",0],["More than 4 million",0],["More than 100 million",0]]},{"g":3378,"t":"What did the repealing of the Corn Laws in 1846 do?","e":"The government began to promote policies of free trade, abolishing a number of taxes on imported goods. One example of this was the repealing of the Corn Laws in 1846. These had prevented the import of cheap grain.","p":1,"o":[["It allowed cheap grain to be imported",1],["It banned the export of British wool",0],["It introduced a tax on imported cotton",0],["It limited factory hours for women and children",0]]},{"g":3379,"t":"In 1847, what limit was placed by law on the working hours of women and children?","e":"Working conditions in factories gradually became better. In 1847, the number of hours that women and children could work was limited by law to 10 hours per day.","p":1,"o":[["10 hours per day",1],["8 hours per day",0],["12 hours per day",0],["6 hours per day",0]]},{"g":3380,"t":"Which TWO engineers pioneered the railway engine just before Victoria came to the throne?","e":"Just before Victoria came to the throne, the father and son George and Robert Stephenson pioneered the railway engine and a major expansion of the railways took place in the Victorian period.","p":1,"o":[["George Stephenson",1],["Robert Stephenson",1],["Isambard Kingdom Brunel",0],["Richard Arkwright",0]]},{"g":3381,"t":"Which railway did Isambard Kingdom Brunel construct?","e":"Brunel was an engineer who built tunnels, bridges, railway lines and ships. He was responsible for constructing the Great Western Railway, which was the first major railway built in Britain. It runs from Paddington Station in London to the south west of England, the West Midlands and Wales.","p":1,"o":[["The Great Western Railway",1],["The London and Glasgow Railway",0],["The Scottish Grand Railway",0],["The East India Railway",0]]},{"g":3382,"t":"Where was the Great Exhibition of 1851 held?","e":"In 1851, the Great Exhibition opened in Hyde Park in the Crystal Palace, a huge building made of steel and glass. Exhibits ranged from huge machines to handmade goods.","p":1,"o":[["In the Crystal Palace, in Hyde Park",1],["In the Royal Albert Hall",0],["In the Tower of London",0],["At the Millennium Dome",0]]},{"g":3383,"t":"Which medal did Queen Victoria introduce during the Crimean War?","e":"From 1853 to 1856, Britain fought with Turkey and France against Russia in the Crimean War. Queen Victoria introduced the Victoria Cross medal during this war. It honours acts of valour by soldiers.","p":1,"o":[["The Victoria Cross",1],["The George Cross",0],["The Order of the Garter",0],["The Nobel Prize",0]]},{"g":3384,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nFlorence Nightingale was born in Italy to English parents.","e":"Florence Nightingale was born in Italy to English parents. At the age of 31, she trained as a nurse in Germany. In 1854, she went to Turkey and worked in military hospitals, treating soldiers who were fighting in the Crimean War.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3385,"t":"Where did Florence Nightingale establish her training school for nurses?","e":"In 1860 she established the Nightingale Training School for nurses at St Thomas' Hospital in London. The school was the first of its kind and still exists today. She is often regarded as the founder of modern nursing.","p":1,"o":[["St Thomas' Hospital, in London",1],["Stoke Mandeville hospital",0],["A military hospital in Turkey",0],["Cambridge University",0]]},{"g":3386,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"The Irish Nationalist movement had grown strongly through the 19th century. Some, such as the Fenians, favoured complete independence. Others, such as Charles Stuart Parnell, advocated 'Home Rule', in which Ireland would remain in the UK but have its own parliament.","p":1,"o":[["The Fenians favoured complete independence for Ireland",1],["The Fenians favoured Home Rule within the UK",0]]},{"g":3387,"t":"What did the Reform Act of 1832 abolish?","e":"The Reform Act of 1832 had greatly increased the number of people with the right to vote. The Act also abolished the old pocket and rotten boroughs and more parliamentary seats were given to the towns and cities.","p":1,"o":[["The old pocket and rotten boroughs",1],["The House of Lords",0],["The property qualification for voting",0],["The Corn Laws",0]]},{"g":3388,"t":"Which TWO changes did the Chartists demand, according to the handbook?","e":"In the 1830s and 1840s, a group called the Chartists campaigned for reform. They wanted six changes: for every man to have the vote, elections every year, for all regions to be equal in the electoral system, secret ballots, for any man to be able to stand as an MP, and for MPs to be paid.","p":2,"o":[["Secret ballots",1],["For MPs to be paid",1],["Votes for women over 30",0],["The abolition of the House of Lords",0]]},{"g":3389,"t":"Emmeline Pankhurst helped found which group in 1903?","e":"Emmeline Pankhurst set up the Women's Franchise League in 1889. In 1903 she helped found the Women's Social and Political Union (WSPU). This was the first group whose members were called 'suffragettes'.","p":1,"o":[["The Women's Social and Political Union",1],["The Women's Franchise League",0],["The Chartists",0],["The Fenians",0]]}]},
    {"n":509,"name":"Toughest Test 9","q":[{"g":3390,"t":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, where are most minor criminal cases dealt with?","e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates' Court. In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court.","p":2,"o":[["In a Magistrates' Court",1],["In a Crown Court",0],["In a Justice of the Peace Court",0],["In a County Court",0]]},{"g":3391,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In England, Wales and Scotland magistrates and Justices of the Peace usually work unpaid and do not need legal qualifications. In Northern Ireland, cases are heard by a District Judge or Deputy District Judge, who is legally qualified and paid.","p":2,"o":[["In Northern Ireland minor criminal cases are heard by a paid, legally qualified District Judge",1],["In Northern Ireland minor criminal cases are heard by unpaid magistrates with no legal qualifications",0]]},{"g":3392,"t":"In Scotland, where are the most serious cases, such as murder, heard?","e":"In Scotland, serious cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury. The most serious cases in Scotland, such as murder, are heard at a High Court with a judge and jury.","p":2,"o":[["At a High Court, with a judge and jury",1],["In a Justice of the Peace Court",0],["In a Crown Court",0],["In the Court of Session",0]]},{"g":3393,"t":"How many members does a jury have in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, and how many in Scotland?","e":"A jury is made up of members of the public chosen at random from the local electoral register. In England, Wales and Northern Ireland a jury has 12 members, and in Scotland a jury has 15 members.","p":2,"o":[["12 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, and 15 in Scotland",1],["15 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, and 12 in Scotland",0],["12 everywhere in the UK",0],["10 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, and 12 in Scotland",0]]},{"g":3394,"t":"Which third verdict is possible in Scotland?","e":"The jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of 'guilty' or 'not guilty' based on what they have heard. In Scotland, a third verdict of 'not proven' is also possible.","p":2,"o":[["Not proven",1],["Not guilty by reason of doubt",0],["Case dismissed",0],["No case to answer",0]]},{"g":3395,"t":"If a jury finds a defendant guilty, who decides the penalty?","e":"The jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of 'guilty' or 'not guilty'. If the jury finds a defendant guilty, the judge decides on the penalty.","p":2,"o":[["The judge",1],["The jury",0],["The police",0],["The magistrates",0]]},{"g":3396,"t":"From where is a jury chosen?","e":"A jury is made up of members of the public chosen at random from the local electoral register. Everyone who is summoned to do jury service must do it unless they are not eligible or they provide a good reason to be excused.","p":2,"o":[["At random from the local electoral register",1],["From a list kept by the police",0],["From volunteers who apply to the court",0],["From members of the local council",0]]},{"g":3397,"t":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, cases involving an accused person of what age are normally heard in a Youth Court?","e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge.","p":2,"o":[["Aged 10 to 17",1],["Aged 14 to 17",0],["Aged 10 to 21",0],["Aged 16 to 18",0]]},{"g":3398,"t":"Which TWO of these are true of Youth Courts?","e":"The parents or carers of the young person are expected to attend the hearing. Members of the public are not allowed in Youth Courts, and the name or photographs of the accused young person cannot be published in newspapers or used by the media.","p":2,"o":[["Members of the public are not allowed in",1],["The young person cannot be named in newspapers",1],["A jury of 12 decides the verdict",0],["Hearings are always broadcast on television",0]]},{"g":3399,"t":"What system is used in Scotland to deal with children and young people who have committed an offence?","e":"In Scotland a system called the Children's Hearings System is used to deal with children and young people who have committed an offence. Northern Ireland has a system of youth conferencing.","p":2,"o":[["The Children's Hearings System",1],["Youth conferencing",0],["The Youth Court",0],["The Justice of the Peace Court",0]]},{"g":3400,"t":"Which court in Scotland deals with most of the civil matters handled by County Courts elsewhere?","e":"County Courts deal with a wide range of civil disputes. These include people trying to get back money that is owed to them, cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract, and divorce. In Scotland, most of these matters are dealt with in the Sheriff Court.","p":2,"o":[["The Sheriff Court",1],["The Court of Session",0],["The High Court",0],["The Justice of the Peace Court",0]]},{"g":3401,"t":"Where are more serious civil cases dealt with in Scotland?","e":"More serious civil cases, for example when a large amount of compensation is being claimed, are dealt with in the High Court in England, Wales and Northern Ireland. In Scotland, they are dealt with in the Court of Session in Edinburgh.","p":2,"o":[["In the Court of Session, in Edinburgh",1],["In the High Court, in Edinburgh",0],["In the Sheriff Court",0],["In a Crown Court",0]]},{"g":3402,"t":"What are the small claims limits in England and Wales, and in Scotland and Northern Ireland?","e":"The small claims procedure is an informal way of helping people to settle minor disputes without spending a lot of time and money using a lawyer. This procedure is used for claims of less than £10,000 in England and Wales and £5,000 in Scotland and Northern Ireland.","p":2,"o":[["Less than £10,000 in England and Wales, and £5,000 in Scotland and Northern Ireland",1],["Less than £5,000 in England and Wales, and £10,000 in Scotland and Northern Ireland",0],["Less than £10,000 everywhere in the UK",0],["Less than £1,000 in England and Wales, and £5,000 in Scotland",0]]},{"g":3403,"t":"Where is a small claims hearing held?","e":"The hearing is held in front of a judge in an ordinary room, and people from both sides of the dispute sit around a table. Small claims can also be issued online through Money Claims Online.","p":2,"o":[["In front of a judge in an ordinary room, with both sides around a table",1],["In a Crown Court in front of a jury",0],["In the House of Lords",0],["In a police station",0]]},{"g":3404,"t":"What do solicitors do?","e":"Solicitors are trained lawyers who give advice on legal matters, take action for their clients and represent their clients in court. Solicitors' charges are usually based on how much time they spend on a case.","p":2,"o":[["Give legal advice, act for clients and represent them in court",1],["Decide the verdict in criminal cases",0],["Set the sentence when a person is found guilty",0],["Appoint judges to the courts",0]]},{"g":3405,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nIf judges find that a public body is not respecting someone's legal rights, they can order that body to change its practices and pay compensation.","e":"Judges are responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly. If judges find that a public body is not respecting someone's legal rights, they can order that body to change its practices and/or pay compensation.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3406,"t":"If judges decide that the actions of the government are illegal, what must the government do?","e":"Sometimes the actions of the government are claimed to be illegal. If the judges agree, then the government must either change its policies or ask Parliament to change the law.","p":2,"o":[["Change its policies or ask Parliament to change the law",1],["Dismiss the judges concerned",0],["Hold a General Election",0],["Refer the matter to the monarch",0]]},{"g":3407,"t":"Which of these is NOT given in the handbook as an example of criminal law?","e":"Examples of criminal laws are carrying a weapon, drugs, racial crime, selling tobacco to those under 18, smoking in public places, buying alcohol for those under 18, and drinking in public where there are alcohol-free zones. Housing disputes between landlords and tenants are an example of civil law.","p":2,"o":[["A dispute between a landlord and a tenant over repairs",1],["Carrying a knife for self-defence",0],["Selling cigarettes to a person aged under 18",0],["Causing distress to someone because of their religion",0]]},{"g":3408,"t":"Who leads the separate police forces in the UK, and to whom are they answerable?","e":"The police are organised into a number of separate police forces headed by Chief Constables. They are independent of the government.","p":2,"o":[["Chief Constables, and the forces are independent of the government",1],["The Home Secretary, who directs each force",0],["Police and Crime Commissioners, who are appointed by the Prime Minister",0],["Local magistrates, who direct each force",0]]},{"g":3409,"t":"What do PCSOs usually do?","e":"Police officers are supported by police community support officers (PCSOs). PCSOs have different roles according to the area but usually patrol the streets, work with the public, and support police officers at crime scenes and major events.","p":2,"o":[["Patrol the streets, work with the public and support officers at crime scenes",1],["Decide which cases go to court",0],["Set the local policing budget",0],["Appoint the Chief Constable",0]]},{"g":3410,"t":"Which TWO independent bodies can receive complaints about the police?","e":"Complaints can also be made to an independent body: the Independent Police Complaints Commission in England and Wales, the Police Complaints Commissioner for Scotland or the Police Ombudsman for Northern Ireland.","p":2,"o":[["The Police Complaints Commissioner for Scotland",1],["The Police Ombudsman for Northern Ireland",1],["The Citizens Advice Bureau",0],["The Equality and Human Rights Commission",0]]},{"g":3411,"t":"What is meant by extremism in the handbook?","e":"It is important that all citizens feel safe. This includes feeling safe from all kinds of extremism, vocal or active opposition to fundamental British values, including religious extremism and far-right extremism.","p":2,"o":[["Vocal or active opposition to fundamental British values",1],["Any strong political opinion",0],["Membership of a pressure group",0],["Voting for a small political party",0]]},{"g":3412,"t":"If someone tries to recruit you to an extremist cause, who should you report it to?","e":"All terrorist groups try to radicalise and recruit people to their cause. If you think someone is trying to persuade you to join an extremist or terrorist cause, you should notify your local police force.","p":2,"o":[["Your local police force",1],["The Citizens Advice Bureau",0],["Your local council",0],["The Independent Police Complaints Commission",0]]},{"g":3413,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nEvery person in the UK receives equal treatment under the law.","e":"Every person in the UK receives equal treatment under the law. This means that the law applies in the same way to everyone, no matter who they are or where they are from.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]}]},
    {"n":510,"name":"Toughest Test 10","q":[{"g":3414,"t":"Whose assassination on 28 June 1914 set off the chain of events leading to the First World War?","e":"On 28 June 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria was assassinated. This set off a chain of events leading to the First World War (1914-18).","p":1,"o":[["Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria",1],["Kaiser Wilhelm of Germany",0],["Tsar Nicholas of Russia",0],["King George V of Britain",0]]},{"g":3415,"t":"Which TWO of these countries fought as part of the Central Powers in the First World War?","e":"The Allies fought against the Central Powers, mainly Germany, the Austro-Hungarian Empire, the Ottoman Empire and later Bulgaria. Britain was part of the Allied Powers, which included France, Russia, Japan, Belgium and Serbia.","p":1,"o":[["Germany",1],["The Ottoman Empire",1],["Russia",0],["Japan",0]]},{"g":3416,"t":"Roughly how many Indians fought on behalf of Britain in the First World War, and how many were killed?","e":"The whole of the British Empire was involved in the conflict. For example, more than a million Indians fought on behalf of Britain in lots of different countries, and around 40,000 were killed.","p":1,"o":[["More than a million fought and around 40,000 were killed",1],["More than 40,000 fought and around 1,000 were killed",0],["More than a million fought and around 400,000 were killed",0],["More than 60,000 fought and around 40,000 were killed",0]]},{"g":3417,"t":"About how many British casualties were there on the first day of the British attack on the Somme in July 1916?","e":"Millions of people were killed or wounded, with more than 2 million British casualties. One battle, the British attack on the Somme in July 1916, resulted in about 60,000 British casualties on the first day alone.","p":1,"o":[["About 60,000",1],["About 6,000",0],["About 300,000",0],["About 40,000",0]]},{"g":3418,"t":"At what time and on what date did the First World War end?","e":"The First World War ended at 11.00 am on 11th November 1918 with victory for Britain and its allies.","p":1,"o":[["At 11.00 am on 11 November 1918",1],["At 11.00 am on 11 November 1914",0],["At midnight on 1 November 1918",0],["At 11.00 am on 6 June 1918",0]]},{"g":3419,"t":"What was the Easter Rising of 1916?","e":"Irish Nationalists were not willing to wait and in 1916 there was an uprising, the Easter Rising, against the British in Dublin. The leaders of the uprising were executed under military law. A guerrilla war against the British army and the police in Ireland followed.","p":1,"o":[["An uprising against the British in Dublin",1],["A Protestant rebellion in Ulster",0],["A strike by Irish farm workers",0],["A naval mutiny in Belfast",0]]},{"g":3420,"t":"In 1922 Ireland became two countries. What were they called?","e":"In 1921 a peace treaty was signed and in 1922 Ireland became two countries. The six counties in the north which were mainly Protestant remained part of the UK under the name Northern Ireland. The rest of Ireland became the Irish Free State. It had its own government and became a republic in 1949.","p":1,"o":[["Northern Ireland and the Irish Free State",1],["Northern Ireland and the Republic of Ireland",0],["Ulster and the Pale",0],["Northern Ireland and Eire Free State",0]]},{"g":3421,"t":"How many counties in the north of Ireland remained part of the UK?","e":"The six counties in the north which were mainly Protestant remained part of the UK under the name Northern Ireland.","p":1,"o":[["Six",1],["Four",0],["Nine",0],["Twelve",0]]},{"g":3422,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"The BBC started radio broadcasts in 1922 and began the world's first regular television service in 1936. The television was developed by Scotsman John Logie Baird, and in 1932 he made the first television broadcast between London and Glasgow.","p":1,"o":[["The BBC began the world's first regular television service in 1936",1],["The BBC began the world's first regular television service in 1922",0]]},{"g":3423,"t":"By roughly how much did car ownership change between 1930 and 1939?","e":"As prices generally fell, those in work had more money to spend. Car ownership doubled from 1 million to 2 million between 1930 and 1939. In addition, many new houses were built.","p":1,"o":[["It doubled, from 1 million to 2 million",1],["It halved, from 2 million to 1 million",0],["It rose from 1 million to 10 million",0],["It stayed the same",0]]},{"g":3424,"t":"Which TWO writers are named in the handbook as prominent in the inter-war cultural blossoming?","e":"It was also a time of cultural blossoming, with writers such as Graham Greene and Evelyn Waugh prominent. The economist John Maynard Keynes published influential new theories of economics.","p":1,"o":[["Graham Greene",1],["Evelyn Waugh",1],["Dylan Thomas",0],["Roald Dahl",0]]},{"g":3425,"t":"What event led Britain and France to declare war in 1939?","e":"The British government tried to avoid another war. However, when Hitler invaded Poland in 1939, Britain and France declared war in order to stop his aggression.","p":1,"o":[["Hitler's invasion of Poland",1],["Hitler's occupation of Austria",0],["The bombing of Pearl Harbour",0],["The German invasion of the Soviet Union",0]]},{"g":3426,"t":"How many men were rescued from the beaches around Dunkirk?","e":"Many civilian volunteers in small pleasure and fishing boats from Britain helped the Navy to rescue more than 300,000 men from the beaches around Dunkirk. The evacuation gave rise to the phrase 'the Dunkirk spirit'.","p":1,"o":[["More than 300,000",1],["More than 30,000",0],["More than 3,000",0],["More than 600,000",0]]},{"g":3427,"t":"Which TWO planes were the most important used by the Royal Air Force in the Battle of Britain?","e":"The most important planes used by the Royal Air Force in the Battle of Britain were the Spitfire and the Hurricane, which were designed and built in Britain.","p":1,"o":[["The Spitfire",1],["The Hurricane",1],["Concorde",0],["The Harrier jump jet",0]]},{"g":3428,"t":"Which city was almost totally destroyed during the Blitz?","e":"Despite this crucial victory, the German air force was able to continue bombing London and other British cities at night-time. This was called the Blitz. Coventry was almost totally destroyed and a great deal of damage was done in other cities, especially in the East End of London.","p":1,"o":[["Coventry",1],["Norwich",0],["Bradford",0],["Aberdeen",0]]},{"g":3429,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Churchill's speech to the House of Commons during the Battle of Britain included the line 'Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few'. His first speech as Prime Minister in 1940 included 'I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat'.","p":1,"o":[["'Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few' was said during the Battle of Britain",1],["'Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few' was said after Dunkirk",0]]},{"g":3430,"t":"In which year was Winston Churchill voted the greatest Briton of all time by the public?","e":"Following his death in 1965, Churchill was given a state funeral. He remains a much-admired figure to this day, and in 2002 was voted the greatest Briton of all time by the public.","p":1,"o":[["2002",1],["1965",0],["1945",0],["1951",0]]},{"g":3431,"t":"On what date did allied forces land in Normandy?","e":"On 6 June 1944, allied forces landed in Normandy, an event often referred to as 'D-Day'. Following victory on the beaches of Normandy, the allied forces pressed on through France and eventually into Germany.","p":1,"o":[["6 June 1944",1],["11 November 1944",0],["6 June 1940",0],["8 May 1945",0]]},{"g":3432,"t":"Which TWO Japanese cities did the United States drop atom bombs on in August 1945?","e":"The war against Japan ended in August 1945 when the United States dropped its newly developed atom bombs on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki.","p":1,"o":[["Hiroshima",1],["Nagasaki",1],["Singapore",0],["Pearl Harbour",0]]},{"g":3433,"t":"Which scientist led the British team that first split the atom?","e":"Scientists led by Ernest Rutherford, working at Manchester and then Cambridge University, were the first to 'split the atom' and took part in the Manhattan Project in the United States, which developed the atomic bomb.","p":1,"o":[["Ernest Rutherford",1],["Alexander Fleming",0],["Isaac Newton",0],["Alan Turing",0]]},{"g":3434,"t":"What did Alexander Fleming discover in 1928, and what was he researching at the time?","e":"Born in Scotland, Fleming moved to London as a teenager and later qualified as a doctor. He was researching influenza, the 'flu', in 1928 when he discovered penicillin. Fleming won the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1945.","p":1,"o":[["Penicillin, while researching influenza",1],["Insulin, while researching diabetes",0],["Penicillin, while researching tuberculosis",0],["Radar, while researching radio waves",0]]},{"g":3435,"t":"Which TWO scientists developed penicillin into a usable drug?","e":"He was researching influenza in 1928 when he discovered penicillin. This was then further developed into a usable drug by the scientists Howard Florey and Ernst Chain. By the 1940s it was in mass production.","p":1,"o":[["Howard Florey",1],["Ernst Chain",1],["Francis Crick",0],["John MacLeod",0]]},{"g":3436,"t":"What is the pledge made by new citizens at a citizenship ceremony based on?","e":"British society is founded on fundamental values and principles which all those living in the UK should respect and support. As part of the citizenship ceremony, new citizens pledge to uphold these values, giving loyalty to the United Kingdom and respecting its rights and freedoms.","p":3,"o":[["The fundamental values and principles of British society",1],["The Bill of Rights of 1689",0],["The Magna Carta",0],["The Beveridge Report",0]]},{"g":3437,"t":"On what are the values reflected in British citizenship based, according to the handbook?","e":"These values are reflected in the responsibilities, rights and privileges of being a British citizen or permanent resident of the UK. They are based on history and traditions and are protected by law, customs and expectations.","p":3,"o":[["History and traditions, protected by law, customs and expectations",1],["A single written constitution",0],["European Union directives",0],["The rules of the Church of England",0]]}]},
    {"n":511,"name":"Toughest Test 11","q":[{"g":3438,"t":"What are European laws called?","e":"The UK joined the European Economic Community in 1973. European laws are called directives, regulations or framework decisions.","p":2,"o":[["Directives, regulations or framework decisions",1],["Conventions and charters",0],["Statutes and by-laws",0],["Acts and orders",0]]},{"g":3439,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nMembership of the Commonwealth is voluntary, and the Commonwealth can suspend a member.","e":"Membership is voluntary. The Commonwealth has no power over its members, although it can suspend membership. The Commonwealth is based on the core values of democracy, good government and the rule of law.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3440,"t":"Which TWO are core values of the Commonwealth, according to the handbook?","e":"The Commonwealth has no power over its members, although it can suspend membership. The Commonwealth is based on the core values of democracy, good government and the rule of law.","p":2,"o":[["Democracy",1],["The rule of law",1],["A common currency",0],["Free movement of workers",0]]},{"g":3441,"t":"On what date was the Treaty of Rome signed, and what did it create?","e":"The European Union, originally called the European Economic Community (EEC), was set up by six western European countries who signed the Treaty of Rome on 25 March 1957.","p":2,"o":[["25 March 1957, creating the European Economic Community",1],["25 March 1973, creating the European Economic Community",0],["25 March 1957, creating the Council of Europe",0],["1 January 1957, creating the European Union",0]]},{"g":3442,"t":"Which TWO countries were among the six founder members of the EEC?","e":"The European Union, originally called the European Economic Community, was set up by six western European countries: Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands.","p":2,"o":[["Luxembourg",1],["Belgium",1],["The United Kingdom",0],["Spain",0]]},{"g":3443,"t":"In which year did the UK become a member of the European Economic Community?","e":"West Germany, France, Belgium, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands formed the European Economic Community (EEC) in 1957. At first the UK did not wish to join the EEC but it eventually did so in 1973.","p":2,"o":[["1973",1],["1957",0],["1950",0],["1969",0]]},{"g":3444,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"The Council of Europe is separate from the EU. It has 47 member countries, including the UK, and is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries. It has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters.","p":2,"o":[["The Council of Europe has no power to make laws",1],["The Council of Europe makes laws that bind its member countries",0]]},{"g":3445,"t":"How many member countries does the Council of Europe have?","e":"The Council of Europe is separate from the EU. It has 47 member countries, including the UK, and is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries.","p":2,"o":[["47",1],["27",0],["15",0],["190",0]]},{"g":3446,"t":"Which convention is the best known drawn up by the Council of Europe?","e":"It has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, the most well-known of which is the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms, usually called the European Convention on Human Rights.","p":2,"o":[["The European Convention on Human Rights",1],["The Treaty of Rome",0],["The Bill of Rights",0],["The Magna Carta",0]]},{"g":3447,"t":"When was the United Nations set up, and what does it aim to do?","e":"The UK is part of the United Nations (UN), an international organisation with more than 190 countries as members. The UN was set up after the Second World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security.","p":2,"o":[["After the Second World War, to prevent war and promote international peace and security",1],["After the First World War, to prevent war and promote international peace",0],["In 1957, to promote trade between European countries",0],["In 1973, to protect human rights in Europe",0]]},{"g":3448,"t":"How many members are there on the UN Security Council, and what is the UK's position?","e":"There are 15 members on the UN Security Council, which recommends action when there are international crises and threats to peace. The UK is one of five permanent members of the Security Council.","p":2,"o":[["15 members, and the UK is one of five permanent members",1],["five members, and the UK is one of 15 permanent members",0],["15 members, and the UK is one of ten permanent members",0],["27 members, and the UK is one of five permanent members",0]]},{"g":3449,"t":"What have NATO members agreed to do?","e":"The UK is also a member of NATO. NATO is a group of European and North American countries that have agreed to help each other if they come under attack. It also aims to maintain peace between all of its members.","p":2,"o":[["To help each other if they come under attack",1],["To use a single currency",0],["To draw up conventions on human rights",0],["To abolish taxes on imported goods",0]]},{"g":3450,"t":"In which year did the UK sign the European Convention on Human Rights?","e":"British diplomats and lawyers had an important role in drafting the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms. The UK was one of the first countries to sign the Convention in 1950.","p":2,"o":[["1950",1],["1998",0],["1689",0],["1957",0]]},{"g":3451,"t":"Which Act incorporated the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law?","e":"The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law. The government, public bodies and the courts must follow the principles of the Convention.","p":2,"o":[["The Human Rights Act 1998",1],["The Habeas Corpus Act 1679",0],["The Bill of Rights 1689",0],["The Education Act 1944",0]]},{"g":3452,"t":"In which THREE documents do British rights have their roots, according to the handbook?","e":"Britain has a long history of respecting an individual's rights and ensuring essential freedoms. These rights have their roots in Magna Carta, the Habeas Corpus Act and the Bill of Rights of 1689.","p":2,"o":[["Magna Carta, the Habeas Corpus Act and the Bill of Rights",1],["The Treaty of Rome, the Beveridge Report and the Human Rights Act",0],["The Domesday Book, Magna Carta and the Act of Union",0],["The Statute of Rhuddlan, the Bill of Rights and the Reform Act",0]]},{"g":3453,"t":"Which TWO of these are principles included in the European Convention on Human Rights?","e":"Some of the principles included in the European Convention on Human Rights are: right to life, prohibition of torture, prohibition of slavery and forced labour, right to liberty and security, right to a fair trial, freedom of thought, conscience and religion, and freedom of expression.","p":2,"o":[["Prohibition of torture",1],["Right to liberty and security",1],["A right to free housing",0],["A right to paid employment",0]]},{"g":3454,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nArranged marriages, where both parties agree to the marriage, are acceptable in the UK.","e":"A marriage should be entered into with the full and free consent of both people involved. Arranged marriages, where both parties agree to the marriage, are acceptable in the UK. Forced marriage is where one or both parties do not or cannot give their consent, and forcing another person to marry is a criminal offence.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3455,"t":"Anyone found to have breached a Forced Marriage Protection Order can be jailed for how long?","e":"A potential victim, or someone acting for them, can apply for an order. Anyone found to have breached an order can be jailed for up to two years for contempt of court.","p":2,"o":[["Up to two years",1],["Up to six months",0],["Up to ten years",0],["Up to five years",0]]},{"g":3456,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Female genital mutilation (FGM), also known as cutting or female circumcision, is illegal in the UK. Practising FGM or taking a girl or woman abroad for FGM is a criminal offence.","p":2,"o":[["Taking a girl or woman abroad for FGM is a criminal offence",1],["FGM is only an offence if it is carried out inside the UK",0]]},{"g":3457,"t":"Which TWO of these does money raised from income tax pay for, according to the handbook?","e":"Money raised from income tax pays for government services such as roads, education, police and the armed forces. National Insurance Contributions pay for state benefits and services such as the state retirement pension and the NHS.","p":2,"o":[["Roads",1],["The armed forces",1],["The state retirement pension",0],["The National Health Service",0]]},{"g":3458,"t":"What is the system called by which most employees have income tax taken from their pay?","e":"For most people, the right amount of income tax is automatically taken from their income from employment by their employer and paid directly to HM Revenue & Customs. This system is called 'Pay As You Earn' (PAYE). If you are self-employed, you need to pay your own tax through a system called 'self-assessment'.","p":2,"o":[["Pay As You Earn",1],["Self-assessment",0],["National Insurance",0],["Money Claims Online",0]]},{"g":3459,"t":"When are young people in the UK sent a National Insurance number?","e":"A National Insurance number is a unique personal account number. It makes sure that the National Insurance Contributions and tax you pay are properly recorded against your name. All young people in the UK are sent a National Insurance number just before their 16th birthday.","p":2,"o":[["Just before their 16th birthday",1],["Just before their 18th birthday",0],["On their 21st birthday",0],["When they first start work",0]]},{"g":3460,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nA National Insurance number on its own proves to an employer that you have the right to work in the UK.","e":"A non-UK national living in the UK and looking for work will need a National Insurance number. However, you can start work without one. A National Insurance number does not on its own prove to an employer that you have the right to work in the UK.","p":2,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3461,"t":"Which TWO of these are freedoms the UK offers, according to the handbook?","e":"In return, the UK offers freedom of belief and religion, freedom of speech, freedom from unfair discrimination, a right to a fair trial, and a right to join in the election of a government.","p":3,"o":[["Freedom of belief and religion",1],["Freedom of speech",1],["Freedom from taxation",0],["Freedom to ignore laws you disagree with",0]]}]},
    {"n":512,"name":"Toughest Test 12","q":[{"g":3462,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In 1945 the British people elected a Labour government. The new Prime Minister was Clement Atlee, who promised to introduce the welfare state outlined in the Beveridge Report. In 1948, Aneurin (Nye) Bevan, the Minister for Health, led the establishment of the National Health Service.","p":1,"o":[["Aneurin Bevan led the establishment of the National Health Service",1],["William Beveridge led the establishment of the National Health Service",0]]},{"g":3463,"t":"Which report provided the basis of the modern welfare state, and in which year was it published?","e":"William Beveridge is best known for the 1942 report Social Insurance and Allied Services, known as the Beveridge Report. The report was commissioned by the wartime government in 1941. It provided the basis of the modern welfare state.","p":1,"o":[["The Beveridge Report, in 1942",1],["The Beveridge Report, in 1948",0],["The Butler Report, in 1944",0],["The Beveridge Report, in 1941",0]]},{"g":3464,"t":"Which TWO of the five 'Giant Evils' named in the Beveridge Report are listed below?","e":"It recommended that the government should find ways of fighting the five 'Giant Evils' of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness and provided the basis of the modern welfare state.","p":1,"o":[["Squalor",1],["Idleness",1],["Poverty",0],["Crime",0]]},{"g":3465,"t":"What did the Education Act 1944, often called 'The Butler Act', introduce?","e":"Richard Austen Butler oversaw the introduction of the Education Act 1944, often called 'The Butler Act', which introduced free secondary education in England and Wales. The division between primary and secondary schools that it enforced still remains in most areas of Britain.","p":1,"o":[["Free secondary education in England and Wales",1],["Free university education throughout the UK",0],["Free school meals for all children",0],["The National Health Service",0]]},{"g":3466,"t":"What phrase describes the protection the new system of benefits was intended to give?","e":"A national system of benefits was also introduced to provide 'social security', so that the population would be protected from the 'cradle to the grave'.","p":1,"o":[["From the cradle to the grave",1],["From dawn to dusk",0],["From the workhouse to the grave",0],["From school to retirement",0]]},{"g":3467,"t":"Which TWO industries were taken into public ownership by the post-war Labour government?","e":"The government took into public ownership (nationalised) the railways, coal mines and gas, water and electricity supplies. Attlee's government undertook the nationalisation of major industries like coal and steel.","p":1,"o":[["The railways",1],["The coal mines",1],["The car industry",0],["The aviation industry",0]]},{"g":3468,"t":"In 1947, independence was granted to how many countries?","e":"Another aspect of change was self-government for former colonies. In 1947, independence was granted to nine countries, including India, Pakistan and Ceylon, now Sri Lanka. Other colonies in Africa, the Caribbean and the Pacific achieved independence over the next 20 years.","p":1,"o":[["Nine",1],["Three",0],["Twenty",0],["Fifty",0]]},{"g":3469,"t":"Which Prime Minister was famous for his 'wind of change' speech about decolonisation?","e":"Britain had a Conservative government from 1951 to 1964. The Prime Minister of the day, Harold Macmillan, was famous for his 'wind of change' speech about decolonisation and independence for the countries of the Empire.","p":1,"o":[["Harold Macmillan",1],["Clement Attlee",0],["Winston Churchill",0],["Margaret Thatcher",0]]},{"g":3470,"t":"In 1948, people from which region were invited to come to the UK and work?","e":"There were labour shortages and the British government encouraged workers from Ireland and other parts of Europe to come to the UK and help with the reconstruction. In 1948, people from the West Indies were also invited to come and work.","p":1,"o":[["The West Indies",1],["Uganda",0],["Australia",0],["Bangladesh",0]]},{"g":3471,"t":"How many people of Indian origin forced to leave Uganda did Britain admit during the early 1970s?","e":"The number of people migrating from the West Indies, India, Pakistan and what is now Bangladesh fell in the late 1960s because the government passed new laws to restrict immigration to Britain. Even so, during the early 1970s, Britain admitted 28,000 people of Indian origin who had been forced to leave Uganda.","p":1,"o":[["28,000",1],["120,000",0],["300,000",0],["40,000",0]]},{"g":3472,"t":"Which TWO well-known pop music groups of the 1960s are named in the handbook?","e":"The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change. It was known as 'the Swinging Sixties'. Two well-known pop music groups at the time were The Beatles and The Rolling Stones.","p":1,"o":[["The Beatles",1],["The Rolling Stones",1],["Monty Python",0],["Morecambe and Wise",0]]},{"g":3473,"t":"Which aircraft did Britain and France develop together?","e":"The 1960s was also a time of technological progress. Britain and France developed the world's only supersonic commercial airliner, Concorde. It first flew in 1969 and began carrying passengers in 1976. Concorde was retired from service in 2003.","p":1,"o":[["Concorde",1],["The Harrier jump jet",0],["The Spitfire",0],["The Hurricane",0]]},{"g":3474,"t":"Who invented the cash-dispensing ATM, and where was the first one put into use?","e":"In the 1960s, James Goodfellow invented the cash-dispensing ATM, automatic teller machine, or 'cashpoint'. The first of these was put into use by Barclays Bank in Enfield, north London in 1967.","p":1,"o":[["James Goodfellow, and the first was at Barclays Bank in Enfield",1],["Sir Christopher Cockerell, and the first was at Barclays Bank in Enfield",0],["James Goodfellow, and the first was at the Bank of England",0],["Sir Frank Whittle, and the first was in Glasgow",0]]},{"g":3475,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Radar was developed by Scotsman Sir Robert Watson-Watt, who proposed that enemy aircraft could be detected by radio waves. The jet engine was developed in Britain in the 1930s by Sir Frank Whittle, a British Royal Air Force engineer officer.","p":1,"o":[["Sir Frank Whittle developed the jet engine",1],["Sir Robert Watson-Watt developed the jet engine",0]]},{"g":3476,"t":"Where was the radio telescope built by Sir Bernard Lovell?","e":"Working with radar led Sir Bernard Lovell to make new discoveries in astronomy. The radio telescope he built at Jodrell Bank in Cheshire was for many years the biggest in the world and continues to operate today.","p":1,"o":[["Jodrell Bank, in Cheshire",1],["Cambridge University",0],["Stoke Mandeville, in Buckinghamshire",0],["Enfield, in north London",0]]},{"g":3477,"t":"Who invented the hovercraft, and in which decade?","e":"Sir Christopher Cockerell, a British inventor, invented the hovercraft in the 1950s.","p":1,"o":[["Sir Christopher Cockerell, in the 1950s",1],["Sir Frank Whittle, in the 1950s",0],["Sir Christopher Cockerell, in the 1930s",0],["Alan Turing, in the 1950s",0]]},{"g":3478,"t":"Which TWO scientists led the team that first cloned a mammal in 1996?","e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell, led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep.","p":1,"o":[["Sir Ian Wilmot",1],["Keith Campbell",1],["Sir Robert Edwards",0],["Patrick Steptoe",0]]},{"g":3479,"t":"On what date was information successfully transferred via the World Wide Web for the first time?","e":"The inventor of the World Wide Web, Sir Tim Berners-Lee, is British. Information was successfully transferred via the web for the first time on 25 December 1990.","p":1,"o":[["25 December 1990",1],["25 December 1996",0],["6 June 1990",0],["25 March 1990",0]]},{"g":3480,"t":"Where was the world's first 'test-tube baby' born, and in which year?","e":"IVF, in-vitro fertilisation, therapy for the treatment of infertility was pioneered in Britain by physiologist Sir Robert Edwards and gynaecologist Patrick Steptoe. The world's first 'test-tube baby' was born in Oldham, Lancashire in 1978.","p":1,"o":[["Oldham, Lancashire, in 1978",1],["Oldham, Lancashire, in 1996",0],["Cambridge, in 1978",0],["Enfield, north London, in 1978",0]]},{"g":3481,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In 1972, the Northern Ireland Parliament was suspended and Northern Ireland was directly ruled by the UK government. Some 3,000 people lost their lives in the decades after 1969 in the violence in Northern Ireland.","p":1,"o":[["The Northern Ireland Parliament was suspended in 1972",1],["The Northern Ireland Parliament was suspended in 1969",0]]},{"g":3482,"t":"For what did Mary Peters win an Olympic gold medal in 1972?","e":"Born in Manchester, Mary Peters moved to Northern Ireland as a child. She was a talented athlete who won an Olympic gold medal in the pentathlon in 1972. She was made a Dame of the British Empire in 2000.","p":1,"o":[["The pentathlon",1],["The heptathlon",0],["Rowing",0],["Ice dancing",0]]},{"g":3483,"t":"Which islands did Argentina invade in 1982?","e":"In 1982, Argentina invaded the Falkland Islands, a British overseas territory in the South Atlantic. A naval taskforce was sent from the UK and military action led to the recovery of the islands.","p":1,"o":[["The Falkland Islands",1],["The Channel Islands",0],["The Isle of Man",0],["The Isle of Wight",0]]},{"g":3484,"t":"Which Prime Minister followed Margaret Thatcher and helped establish the Northern Ireland peace process?","e":"Margaret Thatcher led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990. John Major was Prime Minister after Mrs Thatcher, and helped establish the Northern Ireland peace process.","p":1,"o":[["John Major",1],["Tony Blair",0],["Gordon Brown",0],["Harold Macmillan",0]]},{"g":3485,"t":"Roald Dahl was born in Wales to parents of which nationality?","e":"Roald Dahl was born in Wales to Norwegian parents. He served in the Royal Air Force during the Second World War. His best-known works include Charlie and the Chocolate Factory and George's Marvellous Medicine.","p":1,"o":[["Norwegian",1],["German",0],["Irish",0],["Danish",0]]}]},
    {"n":513,"name":"Toughest Test 13","q":[{"g":3486,"t":"How many national parks are there, and in which parts of the UK?","e":"There are 15 national parks in England, Wales and Scotland. They are areas of protected countryside that everyone can visit, and where people live, work and look after the landscape.","p":0,"o":[["15, in England, Wales and Scotland",1],["15, in England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland",0],["10, in England, Wales and Scotland",0],["33, in England, Wales and Scotland",0]]},{"g":3487,"t":"When was the National Trust founded, and by how many volunteers?","e":"The National Trust was founded in 1895 by three volunteers. There are now more than 61,000 volunteers helping to keep the organisation running.","p":0,"o":[["In 1895, by three volunteers",1],["In 1895, by 61,000 volunteers",0],["In 1984, by three volunteers",0],["In 1927, by three volunteers",0]]},{"g":3488,"t":"What is Big Ben?","e":"Big Ben is the nickname for the great bell of the clock at the Houses of Parliament in London. Many people call the clock Big Ben as well. The clock is over 150 years old and is a popular tourist attraction.","p":0,"o":[["The nickname for the great bell of the clock at the Houses of Parliament",1],["The name of the clock tower at the Houses of Parliament",0],["The largest Ferris wheel in London",0],["The bell of St Paul's Cathedral",0]]},{"g":3489,"t":"Why was the clock tower at the Houses of Parliament named 'Elizabeth Tower'?","e":"The clock tower is named 'Elizabeth Tower' in honour of Queen Elizabeth II's Diamond Jubilee in 2012.","p":0,"o":[["In honour of the Diamond Jubilee in 2012",1],["Because Elizabeth I ordered it to be built",0],["Because it was completed in the reign of Elizabeth I",0],["To mark the millennium",0]]},{"g":3490,"t":"Where is the Eden Project, and what are its biomes like?","e":"The Eden Project is located in Cornwall, in the south west of England. Its biomes, which are like giant greenhouses, house plants from all over the world. The Eden Project is also a charity which runs environmental and social projects internationally.","p":0,"o":[["In Cornwall, and its biomes are like giant greenhouses",1],["In Wales, and its biomes are like giant greenhouses",0],["In Cornwall, and its biomes are underground caves",0],["In Cheshire, and its biomes are like giant greenhouses",0]]},{"g":3491,"t":"The Giant's Causeway is on the coast of which part of the UK, and how was it formed?","e":"Located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland, the Giant's Causeway is a land formation of columns made from volcanic lava. It was formed about 50 million years ago.","p":0,"o":[["Northern Ireland, from volcanic lava",1],["Scotland, from volcanic lava",0],["Northern Ireland, from glacial ice",0],["Wales, from volcanic lava",0]]},{"g":3492,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Loch Lomond is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain and probably the best-known part of the Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park. The Lake District is England's largest national park, and its biggest stretch of water is Windermere.","p":0,"o":[["Loch Lomond is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain",1],["Windermere is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain",0]]},{"g":3493,"t":"On which river is the London Eye, and what was it originally built for?","e":"The London Eye is situated on the southern bank of the River Thames and is a Ferris wheel. It was originally built as part of the UK's celebration of the new millennium and continues to be an important part of New Year celebrations.","p":0,"o":[["The Thames, and it was built for the millennium",1],["The Thames, and it was built for the 2012 Olympic Games",0],["The Severn, and it was built for the millennium",0],["The Thames, and it was built for the Diamond Jubilee",0]]},{"g":3494,"t":"Snowdonia is a national park in which part of the UK, and what is its best-known landmark?","e":"Snowdonia is a national park in North Wales. It covers an area of 838 square miles (2,170 square kilometres). Its most well-known landmark is Snowdon, which is the highest mountain in Wales.","p":0,"o":[["North Wales, and its best-known landmark is Snowdon",1],["Scotland, and its best-known landmark is Snowdon",0],["North Wales, and its best-known landmark is Windermere",0],["The Lake District, and its best-known landmark is Snowdon",0]]},{"g":3495,"t":"Which TWO things can visitors see or do at the Tower of London?","e":"The Tower of London was first built by William the Conqueror after he became king in 1066. Tours are given by the Yeoman Warders, also known as Beefeaters, who tell visitors about the building's history. People can also see the Crown Jewels there.","p":0,"o":[["Take a tour given by the Yeoman Warders",1],["See the Crown Jewels",1],["Ride a Ferris wheel",0],["Visit giant greenhouse biomes",0]]},{"g":3496,"t":"Which lake was voted Britain's favourite view by television viewers in 2007?","e":"The Lake District is England's largest national park. It is famous for its lakes and mountains. The biggest stretch of water is Windermere. In 2007, television viewers voted Wastwater as Britain's favourite view.","p":0,"o":[["Wastwater",1],["Windermere",0],["Loch Lomond",0],["The Trossachs",0]]},{"g":3497,"t":"Which TWO famous gardens named in the handbook are in England?","e":"There are famous gardens to visit throughout the UK, including Kew Gardens, Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England, Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle in Scotland, Bodnant Garden in Wales, and Mount Stewart in Northern Ireland.","p":0,"o":[["Kew Gardens",1],["Sissinghurst",1],["Bodnant Garden",0],["Mount Stewart",0]]},{"g":3498,"t":"Which is England's largest national park, and what is its biggest stretch of water?","e":"The Lake District is England's largest national park. It covers 885 square miles. It is famous for its lakes and mountains. The biggest stretch of water is Windermere.","p":0,"o":[["The Lake District, and Windermere",1],["Snowdonia, and Windermere",0],["The Lake District, and Wastwater",0],["Loch Lomond and the Trossachs, and Windermere",0]]},{"g":3499,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nMost shops in the UK are open seven days a week, although trading hours on Sundays and public holidays are generally reduced.","e":"Most shops in the UK are open seven days a week, although trading hours on Sundays and public holidays are generally reduced. Many towns also have markets on one or more days a week.","p":0,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3500,"t":"At what time do pubs usually open, and what is different on Sundays?","e":"Pubs are usually open during the day from 11.00 am, 12 noon on Sundays. Night clubs with dancing and music usually open and close later than pubs. The licensee decides the hours that the pub or night club is open.","p":0,"o":[["From 11.00 am, but from 12 noon on Sundays",1],["From 12 noon, but from 11.00 am on Sundays",0],["From 10.00 am every day",0],["From 11.00 am every day including Sundays",0]]},{"g":3501,"t":"Who decides the hours that a pub or night club is open?","e":"Pubs are usually open during the day from 11.00 am. Night clubs with dancing and music usually open and close later than pubs. The licensee decides the hours that the pub or night club is open.","p":0,"o":[["The licensee",1],["The local council",0],["The police",0],["The Home Secretary",0]]},{"g":3502,"t":"At 16, under what conditions may a person drink wine or beer?","e":"To buy alcohol in a pub or night club you must be 18 or over. When they are 16, people can drink wine or beer with a meal in a hotel or restaurant, including eating areas in pubs, as long as they are with someone over 18.","p":0,"o":[["With a meal in a hotel or restaurant, with someone over 18",1],["Anywhere, as long as they are with someone over 18",0],["With a meal in a hotel or restaurant, on their own",0],["In a night club, with someone over 18",0]]},{"g":3503,"t":"At what age can a person buy alcohol in a pub or night club?","e":"To buy alcohol in a pub or night club you must be 18 or over, but people under that age may be allowed in some pubs with an adult. When they are 16, people can drink wine or beer with a meal in a hotel or restaurant as long as they are with someone over 18.","p":0,"o":[["18 or over",1],["16 or over",0],["21 or over",0],["17 or over",0]]},{"g":3504,"t":"At what age can a person go into a betting shop or gambling club?","e":"In the UK, people often enjoy a gamble on sports or other events. There are also casinos in many places. You have to be 18 to go into betting shops or gambling clubs.","p":0,"o":[["18",1],["16",0],["21",0],["17",0]]},{"g":3505,"t":"What is the maximum fine for watching TV without a television licence?","e":"Everyone in the UK with a TV, computer or other medium which can be used for watching TV must have a television licence. You will receive a fine of up to £1,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence.","p":0,"o":[["£1,000",1],["£5,000",0],["£100",0],["£10,000",0]]},{"g":3506,"t":"Which TWO groups get help with the cost of a television licence?","e":"People over 75 can apply for a free TV licence and blind people can get a 50% discount. The money from TV licences is used to pay for the British Broadcasting Corporation.","p":0,"o":[["People over 75, who can apply for a free licence",1],["Blind people, who can get a 50% discount",1],["Students, who pay half price",0],["People over 65, who pay nothing",0]]},{"g":3507,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"The BBC is a British public service broadcaster providing television and radio programmes. The BBC is the largest broadcaster in the world. It is the only wholly state-funded media organisation that is independent of government. Other UK channels are primarily funded through advertisements and subscriptions.","p":0,"o":[["The BBC is the only wholly state-funded media organisation that is independent of government",1],["The BBC is funded primarily through advertisements and subscriptions",0]]},{"g":3508,"t":"What must all dogs in public places wear?","e":"It is against the law to treat a pet cruelly or to neglect it. All dogs in public places must wear a collar showing the name and address of the owner. The owner is responsible for keeping the dog under control and for cleaning up after the animal in a public place.","p":0,"o":[["A collar showing the name and address of the owner",1],["A muzzle at all times",0],["A licence disc issued by the council",0],["A collar showing the name of a veterinary surgeon",0]]},{"g":3509,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.","e":"The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland. 'Great Britain' refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland.","p":4,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]}]},
    {"n":514,"name":"Toughest Test 14","q":[{"g":3510,"t":"How many questions are there in the Life in the UK test, and what are they based on?","e":"The test consists of 24 questions about important aspects of life in the UK. Questions are based on ALL parts of the handbook. The 24 questions will be different for each person taking the test at that test session.","p":3,"o":[["24 questions, based on all parts of the handbook",1],["45 questions, based on all parts of the handbook",0],["24 questions, based only on the history chapter",0],["12 questions, based on all parts of the handbook",0]]},{"g":3511,"t":"In which TWO languages, other than English, can special arrangements be made to take the Life in the UK test?","e":"The Life in the UK test is usually taken in English, although special arrangements can be made if you wish to take it in Welsh or Scottish Gaelic.","p":3,"o":[["Welsh",1],["Scottish Gaelic",1],["Irish Gaelic",0],["Scots",0]]},{"g":3512,"t":"Roughly how many registered Life in the UK test centres are there around the UK?","e":"You can only take the test at a registered and approved Life in the UK test centre. There are about 60 test centres around the UK. You can only book your test online.","p":3,"o":[["About 60",1],["About 15",0],["About 600",0],["About 100",0]]},{"g":3513,"t":"What must you take with you to the test?","e":"When booking your test, read the instructions carefully. You will need to take some identification and proof of your address with you to the test. If you don't take these, you will not be able to take the test.","p":3,"o":[["Identification and proof of your address",1],["A copy of the handbook",0],["Your National Insurance number card",0],["A letter from your employer",0]]},{"g":3514,"t":"Which people on work visas normally must pass the Life in the UK test to become permanent residents?","e":"People here on work visas, including those on Tier 1 and Tier 2 of the points based system, normally must pass the Life in the UK test to become permanent residents.","p":3,"o":[["Those on Tier 1 and Tier 2 of the points based system",1],["Only those on Tier 4 student visas",0],["Only citizens of Commonwealth countries",0],["Only those married to a British citizen",0]]},{"g":3515,"t":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, what must you be able to do?","e":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to speak and read English, and have a good understanding of life in the UK.","p":3,"o":[["Speak and read English, and have a good understanding of life in the UK",1],["Speak Welsh and have lived in the UK for ten years",0],["Own property in the UK and pay income tax",0],["Serve in the armed forces and pass a medical test",0]]},{"g":3516,"t":"At what level of English are the Life in the UK test questions written?","e":"The questions are written in a way that requires an understanding of the English language at English for Speakers of Other Languages (ESOL) Entry Level 3, so there is no need to take a separate English language test.","p":3,"o":[["ESOL Entry Level 3",1],["ESOL Entry Level 1",0],["Degree level",0],["ESOL Entry Level 5",0]]},{"g":3517,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Pass an ESOL course in English with Citizenship. You will need to take this course if your standard of English is below ESOL Entry Level 3. The course will help you to improve your English and learn more about life in the UK.","p":3,"o":[["An ESOL course in English with Citizenship is needed if your English is below Entry Level 3",1],["An ESOL course in English with Citizenship is needed only if your English is above Entry Level 3",0]]},{"g":3518,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThere is a fee for submitting an application for permanent residence, and it differs for the various types of application.","e":"Once you have passed one of these tests, you can make an application for permanent residence or British citizenship. There is a fee for submitting an application, which is different for the various types of application.","p":3,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3519,"t":"Which of these is NOT one of the countries that make up the UK?","e":"The UK is made up of England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. The rest of Ireland is an independent country.","p":4,"o":[["The Republic of Ireland",1],["Scotland",0],["Wales",0],["Northern Ireland",0]]},{"g":3520,"t":"Which TWO of these are British overseas territories named in the handbook?","e":"There are also several British overseas territories in other parts of the world, such as St Helena and the Falkland Islands. They are also linked to the UK but are not a part of it.","p":4,"o":[["St Helena",1],["The Falkland Islands",1],["The Isle of Man",0],["The Channel Islands",0]]},{"g":3521,"t":"Where does the parliament that governs the whole UK sit?","e":"The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Westminster. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland also have parliaments or assemblies of their own, with devolved powers in defined areas.","p":4,"o":[["Westminster",1],["Holyrood",0],["Stormont",0],["Cardiff Bay",0]]},{"g":3522,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"There are also several islands which are closely linked with the UK but are not part of it: the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man. These have their own governments and are called 'Crown dependencies'.","p":4,"o":[["The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man have their own governments",1],["The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man are governed from Westminster",0]]},{"g":3523,"t":"Which body would you contact to register to vote?","e":"To be able to vote in a parliamentary, local or European election, you must have your name on the electoral register. If you are eligible to vote, you can register by contacting your local council electoral registration office.","p":2,"o":[["Your local council electoral registration office",1],["The Citizens Advice Bureau",0],["Your local police station",0],["The Independent Police Complaints Commission",0]]},{"g":3524,"t":"When is the electoral register updated each year?","e":"The electoral register is updated every year in September or October. An electoral registration form is sent to every household and this has to be completed and returned with the names of everyone who is resident in the household and eligible to vote.","p":2,"o":[["In September or October",1],["In May",0],["In January",0],["In July",0]]},{"g":3525,"t":"What is different about voter registration in Northern Ireland?","e":"In Northern Ireland a different system operates. This is called 'individual registration' and all those entitled to vote must complete their own registration form. Once registered, people stay on the register provided their personal details do not change.","p":2,"o":[["Individual registration, where everyone completes their own form",1],["Registration is done by the local police",0],["No registration is required",0],["Only householders may register",0]]},{"g":3526,"t":"At what times are polling stations open on election day?","e":"People vote in elections at places called polling stations, or polling places in Scotland. On election day, the polling station or place will be open from 7.00 am until 10.00 pm.","p":2,"o":[["From 7.00 am until 10.00 pm",1],["From 8.00 am until 8.00 pm",0],["From 11.00 am until 10.00 pm",0],["From 7.00 am until 9.00 pm",0]]},{"g":3527,"t":"What must voters in Northern Ireland show at the polling station?","e":"When you arrive at the polling station, the staff will ask for your name and address. In Northern Ireland you will also have to show photographic identification.","p":2,"o":[["Photographic identification",1],["A National Insurance number",0],["A driving licence only",0],["A council tax bill",0]]},{"g":3528,"t":"Which TWO groups of resident citizens can vote in ALL public elections in the UK?","e":"Adult citizens of the UK, and citizens of the Commonwealth and the Irish Republic who are resident in the UK, can vote in all public elections. Adult citizens of other EU states who are resident in the UK can vote in all elections except General Elections.","p":2,"o":[["Commonwealth citizens resident in the UK",1],["Irish Republic citizens resident in the UK",1],["EU citizens resident in the UK",0],["All residents regardless of citizenship",0]]},{"g":3529,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Adult citizens of other EU states who are resident in the UK can vote in all elections except General Elections. Adult citizens of the UK, and citizens of the Commonwealth and the Irish Republic who are resident in the UK, can vote in all public elections.","p":2,"o":[["Resident EU citizens can vote in all elections except General Elections",1],["Resident EU citizens can vote in all elections including General Elections",0]]},{"g":3530,"t":"Which TWO of these groups cannot stand for public office?","e":"Most citizens of the UK, the Irish Republic or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office. There are some exceptions, including members of the armed forces, civil servants, and people found guilty of certain criminal offences.","p":2,"o":[["Members of the armed forces",1],["Civil servants",1],["Teachers",0],["Solicitors",0]]},{"g":3531,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nMembers of the House of Lords may stand for election to the House of Commons.","e":"Members of the House of Lords may not stand for election to the House of Commons but are eligible for all other public offices.","p":2,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3532,"t":"What can you do if it is difficult for you to get to a polling station?","e":"If it is difficult for you to get to a polling station or polling place, you can register for a postal ballot. Your ballot paper will be sent to your home before the election. You then fill it in and post it back.","p":2,"o":[["Register for a postal ballot",1],["Ask a neighbour to vote on your behalf",0],["Vote by telephone",0],["Send your poll card to the council",0]]},{"g":3533,"t":"How much does it cost to enter the public galleries of the Palace of Westminster?","e":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords. You can write to your local MP in advance to ask for tickets or you can queue on the day at the public entrance. Entrance is free.","p":2,"o":[["Nothing, entrance is free",1],["£10 per person",0],["£5 for the Commons and £5 for the Lords",0],["It is free only for UK citizens",0]]}]},
    {"n":515,"name":"Toughest Test 15","q":[{"g":3534,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Jane Austen's books include Pride and Prejudice and Sense and Sensibility. Her novels are concerned with marriage and family relationships. Charles Dickens wrote Oliver Twist and Great Expectations.","p":0,"o":[["Jane Austen wrote Sense and Sensibility",1],["Charles Dickens wrote Sense and Sensibility",0]]},{"g":3535,"t":"Which TWO characters from the books of Charles Dickens are referred to in everyday talk?","e":"Charles Dickens wrote a number of very famous novels, including Oliver Twist and Great Expectations. You will hear references in everyday talk to some of the characters in his books, such as Scrooge, a mean person, or Mr Micawber, always hopeful.","p":0,"o":[["Scrooge",1],["Mr Micawber",1],["Sherlock Holmes",0],["James Bond",0]]},{"g":3536,"t":"Which author wrote Treasure Island, Kidnapped and Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde?","e":"Robert Louis Stevenson (1850-94) wrote books which are still read by adults and children today. His most famous books include Treasure Island, Kidnapped and Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde.","p":0,"o":[["Robert Louis Stevenson",1],["Sir Arthur Conan Doyle",0],["Thomas Hardy",0],["Graham Greene",0]]},{"g":3537,"t":"Whose best-known novels focus on rural society and include Far from the Madding Crowd?","e":"Thomas Hardy (1840-1928) was an author and poet. His best-known novels focus on rural society and include Far from the Madding Crowd and Jude the Obscure.","p":0,"o":[["Thomas Hardy",1],["Evelyn Waugh",0],["Sir Kingsley Amis",0],["Jane Austen",0]]},{"g":3538,"t":"Sir Arthur Conan Doyle was a Scottish doctor best known for creating which character?","e":"Sir Arthur Conan Doyle (1859-1930) was a Scottish doctor and writer. He was best known for his stories about Sherlock Holmes, who was one of the first fictional detectives.","p":0,"o":[["Sherlock Holmes",1],["James Bond",0],["Mr Micawber",0],["Scrooge",0]]},{"g":3539,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Sir Kingsley Amis was an English novelist and poet. He wrote more than 20 novels. The most well-known is Lucky Jim. Evelyn Waugh wrote satirical novels including Decline and Fall and Scoop, and is perhaps best known for Brideshead Revisited.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Kingsley Amis wrote Lucky Jim",1],["Evelyn Waugh wrote Lucky Jim",0]]},{"g":3540,"t":"Which TWO British writers named in the handbook have won the Nobel Prize in Literature?","e":"Several British writers, including the novelist Sir William Golding, the poet Seamus Heaney, and the playwright Harold Pinter, have won the Nobel Prize in Literature.","p":0,"o":[["Sir William Golding",1],["Harold Pinter",1],["Agatha Christie",0],["Ian Fleming",0]]},{"g":3541,"t":"Which novel was voted the country's best-loved novel in 2003?","e":"Agatha Christie's detective stories are read all over the world and Ian Fleming's books introduced James Bond. In 2003, The Lord of the Rings by JRR Tolkien was voted the country's best-loved novel.","p":0,"o":[["The Lord of the Rings",1],["Pride and Prejudice",0],["Brideshead Revisited",0],["Lucky Jim",0]]},{"g":3542,"t":"Since which year has the Man Booker Prize for Fiction been awarded, and to whom?","e":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe. It has been awarded since 1968.","p":0,"o":[["Since 1968, to an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe",1],["Since 1984, to an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe",0],["Since 1968, to any author writing in English",0],["Since 1927, to an author from the UK and Ireland",0]]},{"g":3543,"t":"How many lines must a sonnet have?","e":"As well as plays, Shakespeare wrote many sonnets, poems which must be 14 lines long, and some longer poems.","p":0,"o":[["14",1],["12",0],["16",0],["10",0]]},{"g":3544,"t":"Which Anglo-Saxon poem tells of its hero's battles against monsters?","e":"British poetry is among the richest in the world. The Anglo-Saxon poem Beowulf tells of its hero's battles against monsters and is still translated into modern English.","p":0,"o":[["Beowulf",1],["Sir Gawain and the Green Knight",0],["The Canterbury Tales",0],["Paradise Lost",0]]},{"g":3545,"t":"Which poem from the Middle Ages is about one of the knights at the court of King Arthur?","e":"Poems which survive from the Middle Ages include Chaucer's Canterbury Tales and a poem called Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, about one of the knights at the court of King Arthur.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Gawain and the Green Knight",1],["Beowulf",0],["Paradise Lost",0],["The Bruce",0]]},{"g":3546,"t":"Who wrote Paradise Lost?","e":"As Protestant ideas spread, a number of poets wrote poems inspired by their religious views. One of these was John Milton, who wrote Paradise Lost.","p":0,"o":[["John Milton",1],["William Blake",0],["Lord Byron",0],["John Keats",0]]},{"g":3547,"t":"Which TWO poets were inspired to write about their experiences in the First World War?","e":"Later, many poets, for example Wilfred Owen and Siegfried Sassoon, were inspired to write about their experiences in the First World War. More recently, popular poets have included Sir Walter de la Mare, John Masefield, Sir John Betjeman and Ted Hughes.","p":0,"o":[["Wilfred Owen",1],["Siegfried Sassoon",1],["Ted Hughes",0],["Sir John Betjeman",0]]},{"g":3548,"t":"Where are some of the best-known poets buried or commemorated?","e":"Some of the best-known poets are buried or commemorated in Poet's Corner in Westminster Abbey.","p":0,"o":[["Poet's Corner in Westminster Abbey",1],["St Paul's Cathedral",0],["Lincoln Cathedral",0],["York Minster",0]]},{"g":3549,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"William Wordsworth wrote 'I wander'd lonely as a cloud'. William Blake wrote 'Tyger! Tyger! Burning bright'. Sir Walter Scott wrote poems inspired by Scotland and the traditional stories and songs from the borders.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Walter Scott wrote poems inspired by Scotland and border stories and songs",1],["William Wordsworth wrote poems inspired by Scotland and border stories and songs",0]]},{"g":3550,"t":"Which magazine, first published in the 1840s, was the most famous satirical magazine of the 19th century?","e":"In the 19th century, satirical magazines began to be published. The most famous was Punch, which was published for the first time in the 1840s. Today, magazines such as Private Eye continue the tradition of satire.","p":0,"o":[["Punch",1],["Private Eye",0],["Hansard",0],["Yellow Pages",0]]},{"g":3551,"t":"Which programme introduced a new type of progressive comedy in 1969?","e":"Satire has also continued to be important, with shows like That Was The Week That Was in the 1960s and Spitting Image in the 1980s and 1990s. In 1969, Monty Python's Flying Circus introduced a new type of progressive comedy.","p":0,"o":[["Monty Python's Flying Circus",1],["Spitting Image",0],["That Was The Week That Was",0],["Coronation Street",0]]},{"g":3552,"t":"Who told jokes and made fun of people in the court of medieval kings and rich nobles?","e":"The traditions of comedy and satire, and the ability to laugh at ourselves, are an important part of the UK character. Medieval kings and rich nobles had jesters who told jokes and made fun of people in the Court.","p":0,"o":[["Jesters",1],["Puritans",0],["Yeoman Warders",0],["Pantomime Dames",0]]},{"g":3553,"t":"Which German-born British artist is best known for his portraits?","e":"Lucian Freud (1922-2011) was a German-born British artist. He is best known for his portraits. Henry Moore was an English sculptor best known for large bronze abstract sculptures.","p":0,"o":[["Lucian Freud",1],["Henry Moore",0],["David Hockney",0],["John Petts",0]]},{"g":3554,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Ealing Studios has a claim to being the oldest continuously working film studio facility in the world. Some of the most commercially successful films of all time, including the two highest-grossing film franchises, Harry Potter and James Bond, have been produced in the UK.","p":0,"o":[["Ealing Studios has a claim to being the oldest continuously working film studio in the world",1],["Ealing Studios has a claim to being the largest film studio in the world",0]]},{"g":3555,"t":"Which film directed by David Lean was released in 1962?","e":"Some famous British films include Brief Encounter (1945), directed by David Lean; Lawrence of Arabia (1962), directed by David Lean; and The Third Man (1949), directed by Carol Reed.","p":0,"o":[["Lawrence of Arabia",1],["Brief Encounter",0],["The Third Man",0],["Chariots of Fire",0]]},{"g":3556,"t":"What are the British Academy Film Awards the British equivalent of?","e":"The annual British Academy Film Awards, hosted by the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA), are the British equivalent of the Oscars.","p":0,"o":[["The Oscars",1],["The Turner Prize",0],["The Brit Awards",0],["The Laurence Olivier Awards",0]]},{"g":3557,"t":"Which director left for Hollywood and remained an important film director until his death in 1980?","e":"British studios flourished in the 1930s. Eminent directors included Sir Alexander Korda and Sir Alfred Hitchcock, who later left for Hollywood and remained an important film director until his death in 1980.","p":0,"o":[["Sir Alfred Hitchcock",1],["Sir Alexander Korda",0],["Sir David Lean",0],["Ridley Scott",0]]}]},
    {"n":516,"name":"Toughest Test 16","q":[{"g":3558,"t":"How long ago did Britain become permanently separated from the continent by the Channel?","e":"For much of the Stone Age, Britain was connected to the continent by a land bridge. Britain only became permanently separated from the continent by the Channel about 10,000 years ago. The first farmers arrived in Britain about 6,000 years ago.","p":1,"o":[["About 10,000 years ago",1],["About 6,000 years ago",0],["About 4,000 years ago",0],["About 1,000 years ago",0]]},{"g":3559,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Stonehenge, in what is now the English county of Wiltshire, was probably a special gathering place for seasonal ceremonies. Skara Brae on Orkney is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe.","p":1,"o":[["Skara Brae is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe",1],["Stonehenge is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe",0]]},{"g":3560,"t":"In which county is Stonehenge, and in which island group is Skara Brae?","e":"One of these monuments, Stonehenge, still stands in what is now the English county of Wiltshire. Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe.","p":1,"o":[["Stonehenge is in Wiltshire and Skara Brae is on Orkney",1],["Stonehenge is in Dorset and Skara Brae is on Orkney",0],["Stonehenge is in Wiltshire and Skara Brae is on Iona",0],["Stonehenge is in Suffolk and Skara Brae is on Orkney",0]]},{"g":3561,"t":"In the Bronze Age, what were the tombs in which people buried their dead called?","e":"Around 4,000 years ago, people learned to make bronze. We call this period the Bronze Age. People lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs called round barrows.","p":1,"o":[["Round barrows",1],["Hill forts",0],["Roundhouses",0],["Long barrows",0]]},{"g":3562,"t":"Where can a very impressive Iron Age hill fort still be seen?","e":"People still lived in roundhouses, grouped together into larger settlements, and sometimes defended sites called hill forts. A very impressive hill fort can still be seen today at Maiden Castle, in the English county of Dorset.","p":1,"o":[["Maiden Castle, in Dorset",1],["Skara Brae, on Orkney",0],["Sutton Hoo, in Suffolk",0],["Conwy, in Wales",0]]},{"g":3563,"t":"To which language family did the language of the Iron Age people belong?","e":"Most people were farmers, craft workers or warriors. The language they spoke was part of the Celtic language family. Similar languages were spoken across Europe in the Iron Age, and related languages are still spoken today in some parts of Wales, Scotland and Ireland.","p":1,"o":[["The Celtic language family",1],["The Anglo-Saxon language family",0],["The Norman French language family",0],["The Latin language family",0]]},{"g":3564,"t":"Which TWO things did the Romans bring to Britain, according to the handbook?","e":"The Romans remained in Britain for 400 years. They built roads and public buildings, created a structure of law, and introduced new plants and animals.","p":1,"o":[["A structure of law",1],["New plants and animals",1],["The Celtic language",0],["The feudal system",0]]},{"g":3565,"t":"Who was Boudicca, and where is there a statue of her?","e":"One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England. She is still remembered today and there is a statue of her on Westminster Bridge in London, near the Houses of Parliament.","p":1,"o":[["Queen of the Iceni, with a statue on Westminster Bridge",1],["Queen of the Picts, with a statue on Westminster Bridge",0],["Queen of the Iceni, with a statue in Trafalgar Square",0],["Queen of the Scots, with a statue on Westminster Bridge",0]]},{"g":3566,"t":"Which TWO of these tribes invaded Britain from northern Europe after the Romans left?","e":"Britain was again invaded by tribes from northern Europe: the Jutes, the Angles and the Saxons. The languages they spoke are the basis of modern-day English.","p":1,"o":[["The Jutes",1],["The Angles",1],["The Picts",0],["The Iceni",0]]},{"g":3567,"t":"Which king united the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms in England and defeated the Vikings?","e":"The Anglo-Saxon kingdoms in England united under King Alfred the Great, who defeated the Vikings. Many of the Viking invaders stayed in Britain, especially in the east and north of England, in an area known as the Danelaw.","p":1,"o":[["King Alfred the Great",1],["Cnut, also called Canute",0],["Kenneth MacAlpin",0],["King Harold",0]]},{"g":3568,"t":"Which TWO place names are given in the handbook as coming from the Viking languages?","e":"Many of the Viking invaders stayed in Britain, especially in the east and north of England, in an area known as the Danelaw. Many place names there, such as Grimsby and Scunthorpe, come from the Viking languages.","p":1,"o":[["Grimsby",1],["Scunthorpe",1],["Sutton Hoo",0],["Caernarvon",0]]},{"g":3569,"t":"Who was the first Danish king of England?","e":"Anglo-Saxon kings continued to rule what is now England, except for a short period when there were Danish kings. The first of these was Cnut, also called Canute.","p":1,"o":[["Cnut, also called Canute",1],["Alfred the Great",0],["Kenneth MacAlpin",0],["William the Conqueror",0]]},{"g":3570,"t":"Under which king did the people of the north unite, leading to the term Scotland being used?","e":"In the north, the threat of attack by Vikings had encouraged the people to unite under one king, Kenneth MacAlpin. The term Scotland began to be used to describe that country.","p":1,"o":[["Kenneth MacAlpin",1],["Robert the Bruce",0],["Alfred the Great",0],["Cnut",0]]},{"g":3571,"t":"Which title did Henry VIII take in relation to Ireland?","e":"Henry VII and Henry VIII had extended English control outside the Pale and had established English authority over the whole country. Henry VIII took the title 'King of Ireland'. English laws were introduced and local leaders were expected to follow the instructions of the Lord Lieutenants in Dublin.","p":1,"o":[["King of Ireland",1],["Lord Lieutenant of Ireland",0],["Prince of Ireland",0],["Protector of Ireland",0]]},{"g":3572,"t":"What were the 'plantations' in Ireland?","e":"The English government encouraged Scottish and English Protestants to settle in Ulster, the northern province of Ireland, taking over the land from Catholic landholders. These settlements were known as plantations. Many of the new settlers came from south-west Scotland.","p":1,"o":[["Settlements of Scottish and English Protestants on land taken from Catholic landholders",1],["Large farms growing potatoes for export",0],["Forests planted to supply timber for the navy",0],["Castles built to defend the Pale",0]]},{"g":3573,"t":"Who mapped the coast of Australia during the period of increased colonisation?","e":"This was also a time of increased colonisation overseas. Captain James Cook mapped the coast of Australia and a few colonies were established there. Britain gained control over Canada, and the East India Company, originally set up to trade, gained control of large parts of India.","p":1,"o":[["Captain James Cook",1],["Sir Francis Drake",0],["Admiral Nelson",0],["Sir Francis Chichester",0]]},{"g":3574,"t":"What was the East India Company originally set up to do?","e":"Britain gained control over Canada, and the East India Company, originally set up to trade, gained control of large parts of India. Colonies began to be established in southern Africa.","p":1,"o":[["To trade",1],["To govern India",0],["To transport slaves",0],["To map the coast of Australia",0]]},{"g":3575,"t":"Which TWO goods came to Britain from North America and the West Indies?","e":"Britain traded all over the world and began to import more goods. Sugar and tobacco came from North America and the West Indies; textiles, tea and spices came from India and the area that is today called Indonesia.","p":1,"o":[["Sugar",1],["Tobacco",1],["Tea",0],["Spices",0]]},{"g":3576,"t":"What was Richard Arkwright's original trade before he worked in textiles?","e":"Born in 1732, Arkwright originally trained and worked as a barber. He was able to dye hair and make wigs. When wigs became less popular, he started to work in textiles. He improved the original carding machine.","p":1,"o":[["A barber",1],["A weaver",0],["An engineer",0],["A miner",0]]},{"g":3577,"t":"What is carding, the process Arkwright's improved machine performed?","e":"He improved the original carding machine. Carding is the process of preparing fibres for spinning into yarn and fabric. He also developed horse-driven spinning mills that used only one machine.","p":1,"o":[["Preparing fibres for spinning into yarn and fabric",1],["Weaving cloth on a mechanical loom",0],["Dyeing wool before it is woven",0],["Mass producing steel",0]]},{"g":3578,"t":"What did Sake Dean Mahomet open in George Street, London in 1810?","e":"In 1810 he opened the Hindoostane Coffee House in George Street, London. It was the first curry house to open in Britain. Mahomet and his wife also introduced 'shampooing', the Indian art of head massage, to Britain.","p":1,"o":[["The Hindoostane Coffee House, the first curry house in Britain",1],["The first Indian textile mill in Britain",0],["The first cricket club in Britain",0],["The first tennis club in Britain",0]]},{"g":3579,"t":"Which process for the mass production of steel led to the development of shipbuilding and the railways?","e":"The development of the Bessemer process for the mass production of steel led to the development of the shipbuilding industry and the railways. Manufacturing jobs became the main source of employment in Britain.","p":1,"o":[["The Bessemer process",1],["The carding process",0],["The Watt process",0],["The Brunel process",0]]},{"g":3580,"t":"Against whom did Britain fight the Boer War, and where?","e":"The Boer War of 1899 to 1902 made the discussions about the future of the Empire more urgent. The British went to war in South Africa with settlers from the Netherlands called the Boers. The Boers fought fiercely and the war went on for over three years.","p":1,"o":[["Settlers from the Netherlands, in South Africa",1],["Settlers from Germany, in South Africa",0],["Settlers from the Netherlands, in Canada",0],["Settlers from France, in South Africa",0]]},{"g":3581,"t":"In which year was Rudyard Kipling awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature?","e":"Rudyard Kipling was born in India in 1865 and later lived in India, the UK and the USA. His poems and novels reflected the idea that the British Empire was a force for good. Kipling was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1907.","p":1,"o":[["1907",1],["1865",0],["1945",0],["1968",0]]}]},
    {"n":517,"name":"Toughest Test 17","q":[{"g":3582,"t":"Who does the monarch invite to become Prime Minister?","e":"The monarch invites the leader of the party with the largest number of MPs, or the leader of a coalition between more than one party, to become the Prime Minister.","p":2,"o":[["The leader of the party with the largest number of MPs, or of a coalition",1],["The leader of the second-largest party",0],["The Speaker of the House of Commons",0],["The longest-serving member of the House of Lords",0]]},{"g":3583,"t":"Which THREE things may the monarch do in meetings with the Prime Minister?","e":"The monarch has regular meetings with the Prime Minister and can advise, warn and encourage, but the decisions on government policies are made by the Prime Minister and cabinet.","p":2,"o":[["Advise, warn and encourage",1],["Decide, direct and command",0],["Appoint, dismiss and replace",0],["Debate, amend and veto",0]]},{"g":3584,"t":"What does the monarch do at the opening of the new parliamentary session each year?","e":"The monarch has important ceremonial roles, such as the opening of the new parliamentary session each year. On this occasion the monarch makes a speech which summarises the government's policies for the year ahead.","p":2,"o":[["Makes a speech summarising the government's policies for the year ahead",1],["Appoints the Speaker of the House of Commons",0],["Chooses the members of the cabinet",0],["Announces the date of the next General Election",0]]},{"g":3585,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nAll Acts of Parliament are made in the monarch's name.","e":"The monarch has important ceremonial roles, such as the opening of the new parliamentary session each year. All Acts of Parliament are made in the monarch's name.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3586,"t":"What is the National Anthem of the UK?","e":"The National Anthem of the UK is 'God Save the King (or Queen)'. It is played at important national occasions and at events attended by the King or Queen or the Royal Family.","p":2,"o":[["God Save the King",1],["Land of Hope and Glory",0],["I Vow to Thee, My Country",0],["Auld Lang Syne",0]]},{"g":3587,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"New citizens swear or affirm loyalty as part of the citizenship ceremony. The oath of allegiance begins 'I swear by Almighty God', while the affirmation of allegiance begins 'I do solemnly, sincerely and truly declare and affirm'.","p":2,"o":[["New citizens may either swear an oath or make an affirmation of allegiance",1],["New citizens must swear an oath by Almighty God with no alternative",0]]},{"g":3588,"t":"About how many senior MPs does the Prime Minister appoint as ministers in charge of departments?","e":"The Prime Minister appoints about 20 senior MPs to become ministers in charge of departments. These ministers form the cabinet, a committee which usually meets weekly and makes important decisions about government policy.","p":2,"o":[["About 20",1],["About 60",0],["About 129",0],["About 100",0]]},{"g":3589,"t":"How often does the cabinet usually meet?","e":"These ministers form the cabinet, a committee which usually meets weekly and makes important decisions about government policy. Many of these decisions have to be debated or approved by Parliament.","p":2,"o":[["Weekly",1],["Daily",0],["Monthly",0],["Once a year",0]]},{"g":3590,"t":"Which TWO junior ministerial roles exist in each government department?","e":"Each department also has a number of other ministers, called Ministers of State and Parliamentary Under-Secretaries of State, who take charge of particular areas of the department's work.","p":2,"o":[["Ministers of State",1],["Parliamentary Under-Secretaries of State",1],["Shadow ministers",0],["Chief Constables",0]]},{"g":3591,"t":"What are the two official homes of the Prime Minister?","e":"The official home of the Prime Minister is 10 Downing Street, in central London, near the Houses of Parliament. He or she also has a country house outside London called Chequers.","p":2,"o":[["10 Downing Street and Chequers",1],["Buckingham Palace and Chequers",0],["10 Downing Street and Holyrood",0],["The Palace of Westminster and Chequers",0]]},{"g":3592,"t":"Which party in the House of Commons is called the opposition?","e":"The second-largest party in the House of Commons is called the opposition. The leader of the opposition usually becomes Prime Minister if his or her party wins the next General Election.","p":2,"o":[["The second-largest party",1],["The party with the most MPs",0],["Any party that is not in the cabinet",0],["The party with the fewest MPs",0]]},{"g":3593,"t":"How often does Prime Minister's Questions take place?","e":"The leader of the opposition leads his or her party in pointing out what they see as the government's failures and weaknesses. One important opportunity to do this is at Prime Minister's Questions, which takes place every week while Parliament is sitting.","p":2,"o":[["Every week while Parliament is sitting",1],["Every day while Parliament is sitting",0],["Once a month",0],["Once a year",0]]},{"g":3594,"t":"What is the role of the shadow cabinet?","e":"The leader of the opposition also appoints senior opposition MPs to be 'shadow ministers'. They form the shadow cabinet and their role is to challenge the government and put forward alternative policies.","p":2,"o":[["To challenge the government and put forward alternative policies",1],["To advise the Prime Minister on policy",0],["To chair debates in the House of Commons",0],["To appoint civil servants",0]]},{"g":3595,"t":"What are MPs who do not represent any of the main political parties called?","e":"There are a few MPs who do not represent any of the main political parties. They are called 'independents' and usually represent an issue important to their constituency.","p":2,"o":[["Independents",1],["Life peers",0],["Shadow ministers",0],["Backbenchers",0]]},{"g":3596,"t":"Which TWO organisations are given as examples of pressure and lobby groups?","e":"Pressure and lobby groups are organisations which try to influence government policy. Some are representative organisations such as the CBI, which represents the views of British business. Others campaign on particular topics, such as the environment, for example Greenpeace, or human rights, for example Liberty.","p":2,"o":[["Greenpeace",1],["Liberty",1],["The Citizens Advice Bureau",0],["The National Trust",0]]},{"g":3597,"t":"Which organisation represents the views of British business?","e":"Some are representative organisations such as the CBI (Confederation of British Industry), which represents the views of British business.","p":2,"o":[["The CBI",1],["Liberty",0],["Greenpeace",0],["The BBC",0]]},{"g":3598,"t":"Which TWO are core values of the civil service, according to the handbook?","e":"Civil servants are expected to carry out their role with dedication and a commitment to the civil service and its core values. These are: integrity, honesty, objectivity and impartiality, including being politically neutral.","p":2,"o":[["Integrity",1],["Impartiality",1],["Loyalty to the governing party",0],["Seniority",0]]},{"g":3599,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nCivil servants are political appointees chosen by the governing party.","e":"Civil servants support the government in developing and implementing its policies. Civil servants are accountable to ministers. They are chosen on merit and are politically neutral: they are not political appointees.","p":2,"o":[["FALSE",1],["TRUE",0]]},{"g":3600,"t":"How are local authorities funded?","e":"Towns, cities and rural areas in the UK are governed by democratically elected councils, often called 'local authorities'. Local authorities provide a range of services in their areas. They are funded by money from central government and by local taxes.","p":2,"o":[["By money from central government and by local taxes",1],["Entirely by local taxes",0],["Entirely by central government",0],["By the television licence fee",0]]},{"g":3601,"t":"How many local authorities does London have, and what coordinates policies across the capital?","e":"London has 33 local authorities, with the Greater London Authority and the Mayor of London coordinating policies across the capital.","p":2,"o":[["33 local authorities, coordinated by the Greater London Authority and the Mayor of London",1],["60 local authorities, coordinated by the Greater London Authority",0],["33 local authorities, coordinated by the Home Secretary",0],["15 local authorities, coordinated by the Mayor of London",0]]},{"g":3602,"t":"In which month are local elections for councillors usually held?","e":"Many local authorities appoint a mayor, who is the ceremonial leader of the council. For most local authorities, local elections for councillors are held in May every year.","p":2,"o":[["May",1],["September",0],["January",0],["November",0]]},{"g":3603,"t":"Since which year have powers been devolved from central government to Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland?","e":"Since 1997, some powers have been devolved from the central government to give people in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland more control over matters that directly affect them. There has been a Welsh Assembly and a Scottish Parliament since 1999.","p":2,"o":[["1997",1],["1999",0],["1922",0],["2007",0]]},{"g":3604,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe devolved administrations each have their own civil service.","e":"Policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security all remain under central UK government control. However, many other public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations. The devolved administrations each have their own civil service.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3605,"t":"Where does the Welsh Assembly meet?","e":"In Wales the elected members, known as AMs, meet in the Welsh Assembly in the Senedd in Cardiff Bay.","p":2,"o":[["The Senedd, in Cardiff Bay",1],["Holyrood, in Edinburgh",0],["Stormont, in Belfast",0],["The Palace of Westminster",0]]}]},
    {"n":518,"name":"Toughest Test 18","q":[{"g":3606,"t":"In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, what percentage of people identified themselves as Christian?","e":"The UK is historically a Christian country. In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, 70% of people identified themselves as Christian. In the Citizenship Survey, 21% of people said that they had no religion.","p":0,"o":[["70%",1],["84%",0],["21%",0],["50%",0]]},{"g":3607,"t":"In the Citizenship Survey, what percentage of people said that they had no religion?","e":"Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4%), Hindu (2%), Sikh (1%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%), and 2% of people followed another religion. In the Citizenship Survey, 21% of people said that they had no religion.","p":0,"o":[["21%",1],["70%",0],["4%",0],["2%",0]]},{"g":3608,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4%), Hindu (2%), Sikh (1%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%).","p":0,"o":[["A larger proportion identified themselves as Muslim than as Hindu",1],["A larger proportion identified themselves as Hindu than as Muslim",0]]},{"g":3609,"t":"Which TWO places of worship are named in the handbook for religions other than Christianity?","e":"There are religious buildings for other religions all over the UK. This includes Islamic mosques, Hindu temples, Jewish synagogues, Sikh gurdwaras and Buddhist temples.","p":0,"o":[["Sikh gurdwaras",1],["Jewish synagogues",1],["Anglican cathedrals",0],["Presbyterian kirks",0]]},{"g":3610,"t":"Since when has the Church of England existed?","e":"The official Church of the state is the Church of England. It is a Protestant Church and has existed since the Reformation in the 1530s.","p":0,"o":[["Since the Reformation in the 1530s",1],["Since the Norman Conquest of 1066",0],["Since the Restoration of 1660",0],["Since the Glorious Revolution of 1688",0]]},{"g":3611,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nSeveral Church of England bishops sit in the House of Lords.","e":"The monarch is the head of the Church of England. The spiritual leader of the Church of England is the Archbishop of Canterbury. Several Church of England bishops sit in the House of Lords.","p":0,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3612,"t":"What was the population of the UK in 1901, according to the handbook's table?","e":"The table of population growth in the UK shows: 1801, 8 million; 1851, 20 million; 1901, 40 million; 1951, 50 million; 1998, 57 million.","p":0,"o":[["40 million",1],["20 million",0],["8 million",0],["50 million",0]]},{"g":3613,"t":"According to the handbook's table, what was the population of the UK in 1851?","e":"The table of population growth in the UK shows: 1600, just over 4 million; 1700, 5 million; 1801, 8 million; 1851, 20 million; 1901, 40 million.","p":0,"o":[["20 million",1],["8 million",0],["40 million",0],["5 million",0]]},{"g":3614,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8% and Northern Ireland less than 3%.","p":0,"o":[["Wales has a larger share of the UK population than Northern Ireland",1],["Northern Ireland has a larger share of the UK population than Wales",0]]},{"g":3615,"t":"Why are people in the UK living longer than ever before?","e":"People in the UK are living longer than ever before. This is due to improved living standards and better health care. There are now a record number of people aged 85 and over. This has an impact on the cost of pensions and health care.","p":0,"o":[["Improved living standards and better health care",1],["A fall in the birth rate",0],["Increased migration into the UK",0],["Shorter working hours",0]]},{"g":3616,"t":"In surveys, which is the most common ethnic description chosen?","e":"In surveys, the most common ethnic description chosen is white, which includes people of European, Australian, Canadian, New Zealand and American descent. Other significant groups are those of Asian, black and mixed descent.","p":0,"o":[["White",1],["Asian",0],["Black",0],["Mixed",0]]},{"g":3617,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Women in Britain today make up about half of the workforce. On average, girls leave school with better qualifications than boys. More women than men study at university.","p":0,"o":[["More women than men study at university",1],["More men than women study at university",0]]},{"g":3618,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nIn the UK it is a legal requirement that men and women should not be discriminated against because of their gender or because they are, or are not, married.","e":"Within the UK, it is a legal requirement that men and women should not be discriminated against because of their gender or because they are, or are not, married. They have equal rights to work, own property, marry and divorce.","p":0,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3619,"t":"Which day comes immediately before Lent begins?","e":"The day before Lent starts is called Shrove Tuesday, or Pancake Day. People eat pancakes, which were traditionally made to use up foods such as eggs, fat and milk before fasting. Lent begins on Ash Wednesday.","p":0,"o":[["Shrove Tuesday",1],["Ash Wednesday",0],["Good Friday",0],["Easter Monday",0]]},{"g":3620,"t":"How many days before Easter does Lent last?","e":"The 40 days before Easter are known as Lent. It is a time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter. Traditionally, people would fast during this period and today many people will give something up.","p":0,"o":[["40 days",1],["30 days",0],["8 days",0],["five days",0]]},{"g":3621,"t":"Which TWO days at Easter are public holidays?","e":"Easter takes place in March or April. It marks the death of Jesus Christ on Good Friday and his rising from the dead on Easter Sunday. Both Good Friday and the following Monday, called Easter Monday, are public holidays.","p":0,"o":[["Good Friday",1],["Easter Monday",1],["Easter Sunday",0],["Shrove Tuesday",0]]},{"g":3622,"t":"When is Mothering Sunday?","e":"Mothering Sunday, or Mother's Day, is the Sunday three weeks before Easter. Children send cards or buy gifts for their mothers. Father's Day is the third Sunday in June.","p":0,"o":[["The Sunday three weeks before Easter",1],["The third Sunday in June",0],["The first Sunday in May",0],["The Sunday after Easter",0]]},{"g":3623,"t":"How long does Hannukah last, and what does it remember?","e":"Hannukah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days. It is to remember the Jews' struggle for religious freedom. On each day of the festival a candle is lit on a stand of eight candles, called a menorah.","p":0,"o":[["Eight days, remembering the Jews' struggle for religious freedom",1],["Five days, remembering the Jews' struggle for religious freedom",0],["Eight days, remembering the founding of the Khalsa",0],["Eight days, remembering the end of Ramadan",0]]},{"g":3624,"t":"On which date is Vaisakhi celebrated?","e":"Vaisakhi, also spelled Baisakhi, is a Sikh festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa. It is celebrated on 14 April each year with parades, dancing and singing.","p":0,"o":[["14 April",1],["14 February",0],["1 April",0],["5 November",0]]},{"g":3625,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In Scotland, 31 December is called Hogmanay and 2 January is also a public holiday. For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas.","p":0,"o":[["In Scotland, 2 January is also a public holiday",1],["In Scotland, 2 January is an ordinary working day",0]]},{"g":3626,"t":"Until what time of day do people play jokes on April Fool's Day?","e":"April Fool's Day, 1 April, is a day when people play jokes on each other until midday. The television and newspapers often have stories that are April Fool jokes.","p":0,"o":[["Until midday",1],["Until midnight",0],["Until 11.00 am",0],["All day",0]]},{"g":3627,"t":"What happened in 1605 that Bonfire Night commemorates?","e":"Bonfire Night, 5 November, is an occasion when people in Great Britain set off fireworks. The origin of this celebration was an event in 1605, when a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in their plan to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament.","p":0,"o":[["A group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in a plan to kill the Protestant king",1],["A group of Protestants failed in a plan to kill the Catholic king",0],["The Houses of Parliament burned down",0],["The Gunpowder was discovered at the Tower of London",0]]},{"g":3628,"t":"Where are wreaths laid on Remembrance Day?","e":"Remembrance Day, 11 November, commemorates those who died fighting for the UK and its allies. People wear poppies. At 11.00 am there is a two-minute silence and wreaths are laid at the Cenotaph in Whitehall, London.","p":0,"o":[["At the Cenotaph in Whitehall, London",1],["At Westminster Abbey",0],["At the Tower of London",0],["At St Paul's Cathedral",0]]},{"g":3629,"t":"Which additional public holiday is marked in Northern Ireland in July?","e":"There are other public holidays each year called bank holidays. These are of no religious significance. They are at the beginning of May, in late May or early June, and in August. In Northern Ireland, the anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne in July is also a public holiday.","p":0,"o":[["The anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne",1],["St Patrick's Day",0],["The anniversary of the Easter Rising",0],["Hogmanay",0]]}]},
    {"n":519,"name":"Toughest Test 19","q":[{"g":3630,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England. During the reign of Henry VIII, Wales became formally united with England by the Act for the Government of Wales, and the Welsh sent representatives to the House of Commons.","p":1,"o":[["Wales became formally united with England by the Act for the Government of Wales",1],["Wales became formally united with England by the Statute of Rhuddlan",0]]},{"g":3631,"t":"Which TWO castles were built to maintain English power in Wales?","e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England. Huge castles, including Conwy and Caernarvon, were built to maintain this power.","p":1,"o":[["Conwy",1],["Caernarvon",1],["Windsor",0],["Edinburgh",0]]},{"g":3632,"t":"By 1400, in what language were official documents in England being written?","e":"Gradually these two languages combined to become one English language. By 1400, in England, official documents were being written in English, and English had become the preferred language of the royal court and Parliament.","p":1,"o":[["English",1],["Norman French",0],["Latin",0],["Anglo-Saxon",0]]},{"g":3633,"t":"Which work by Geoffrey Chaucer was one of the first books to be printed by William Caxton?","e":"In the years leading up to 1400, Geoffrey Chaucer wrote a series of poems in English about a group of people going to Canterbury on a pilgrimage. This collection of poems is called The Canterbury Tales. It was one of the first books to be printed by William Caxton.","p":1,"o":[["The Canterbury Tales",1],["The Bruce",0],["Beowulf",0],["Sir Gawain and the Green Knight",0]]},{"g":3634,"t":"Which TWO cathedrals are named in the handbook in connection with the Middle Ages?","e":"Great cathedrals, for example Lincoln Cathedral, were also built, and many of these are still used for worship. Several of the cathedrals had windows of stained glass. The glass in York Minster is a famous example.","p":1,"o":[["Lincoln Cathedral",1],["York Minster",1],["St Paul's Cathedral",0],["Westminster Abbey",0]]},{"g":3635,"t":"What became a very important English export during the Middle Ages?","e":"During this period, England was an important trading nation. English wool became a very important export. People came to England from abroad to trade and also to work.","p":1,"o":[["Wool",1],["Steel",0],["Coal",0],["Cotton cloth",0]]},{"g":3636,"t":"Which skilled workers came to England from Holland during the Middle Ages?","e":"Many had special skills, such as weavers from France, engineers from Germany, glass manufacturers from Italy and canal builders from Holland.","p":1,"o":[["Canal builders",1],["Weavers",0],["Engineers",0],["Glass manufacturers",0]]},{"g":3637,"t":"Which TWO castles from the Middle Ages are still in use today, according to the handbook?","e":"Castles were built in many places in Britain and Ireland, partly for defence. Today many are in ruins, although some, such as Windsor and Edinburgh, are still in use.","p":1,"o":[["Windsor",1],["Edinburgh",1],["Conwy",0],["Caernarvon",0]]},{"g":3638,"t":"Under feudalism, what did the king give to his lords, and what did he receive in return?","e":"The Normans used a system of land ownership known as feudalism. The king gave land to his lords in return for help in war. Landowners had to send certain numbers of men to serve in the army.","p":1,"o":[["He gave land in return for help in war",1],["He gave money in return for taxes",0],["He gave castles in return for wool",0],["He gave titles in return for land",0]]},{"g":3639,"t":"In which parts of Britain and Ireland was land owned by 'clans' rather than under feudalism?","e":"Some peasants had their own land but most were serfs. The same system developed in southern Scotland. In the north of Scotland and Ireland, land was owned by members of the 'clans', prominent families.","p":1,"o":[["The north of Scotland and Ireland",1],["Southern Scotland and Wales",0],["The north of England",0],["Ireland only",0]]},{"g":3640,"t":"Which TWO new social groups emerged following the Black Death?","e":"Following the Black Death, the smaller population meant there was less need to grow cereal crops. There were labour shortages and peasants began to demand higher wages. New social classes appeared, including owners of large areas of land, later called the gentry, and people left the countryside to live in the towns. In the towns, growing wealth led to the development of a strong middle class.","p":1,"o":[["The gentry",1],["A strong middle class",1],["The serfs",0],["The clans",0]]},{"g":3641,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"This war was called the Wars of the Roses, because the symbol of Lancaster was a red rose and the symbol of York was a white rose. The war ended with the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485.","p":1,"o":[["The symbol of the House of Lancaster was a red rose",1],["The symbol of the House of Lancaster was a white rose",0]]},{"g":3642,"t":"Whom did Henry VII marry in order to unite the two families after Bosworth Field?","e":"King Richard III of the House of York was killed in the battle and Henry Tudor, the leader of the House of Lancaster, became King Henry VII. Henry then married King Richard's niece, Elizabeth of York, and united the two families.","p":1,"o":[["Elizabeth of York, King Richard's niece",1],["Mary, Queen of Scots",0],["Catherine of Aragon",0],["Anne Boleyn",0]]},{"g":3643,"t":"What was the symbol of the House of Tudor?","e":"Henry was the first king of the House of Tudor. The symbol of the House of Tudor was a red rose with a white rose inside it as a sign that the Houses of York and Lancaster were now allies.","p":1,"o":[["A red rose with a white rose inside it",1],["A white rose with a red rose inside it",0],["A red rose alone",0],["A white rose alone",0]]},{"g":3644,"t":"Which TWO things did Henry VII do after winning the Wars of the Roses?","e":"Henry VII won the Wars of the Roses, curbed the power of the nobles and built up the treasury. When he died, his son Henry VIII continued centralising power.","p":1,"o":[["He curbed the power of the nobles",1],["He built up the treasury",1],["He created the Church of England",0],["He annexed Wales to the Crown of England",0]]},{"g":3645,"t":"Which of these did Protestants do, according to the handbook's account of the Reformation?","e":"The Protestants formed their own churches. They read the Bible in their own languages instead of in Latin; they did not pray to saints or at shrines; and they believed that a person's own relationship with God was more important than submitting to the authority of the Church.","p":1,"o":[["They read the Bible in their own languages instead of in Latin",1],["They prayed to saints and at shrines",0],["They accepted the authority of the Pope",0],["They read the Bible only in Latin",0]]},{"g":3646,"t":"What is the King James Version of the Bible also known as?","e":"One achievement of King James' reign was a new translation of the Bible into English. This translation is known as the 'King James Version' or the 'Authorised Version'. It was not the first English Bible but is a version which continues to be used in many Protestant churches today.","p":1,"o":[["The Authorised Version",1],["The Book of Common Prayer",0],["The Great Bible",0],["The Geneva Bible",0]]},{"g":3647,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nWhen James VI of Scotland became King James I of England, Scotland remained a separate country.","e":"Elizabeth I never married and so had no children of her own to inherit her throne. When she died in 1603 her heir was her cousin James VI of Scotland. He became King James I of England, Wales and Ireland but Scotland remained a separate country.","p":1,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3648,"t":"Who were the Puritans?","e":"Many in Parliament were Puritans, a group of Protestants who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship. They did not agree with the king's religious views and disliked his reforms of the Church of England.","p":1,"o":[["A group of Protestants who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship",1],["A group of Catholics who supported the Pope",0],["Supporters of the king in the civil war",0],["Followers of Oliver Cromwell in Ireland",0]]},{"g":3649,"t":"Which battle did William III win in Ireland in 1690?","e":"James II wanted to regain the throne and invaded Ireland with the help of a French army. William defeated James II at the Battle of the Boyne in Ireland in 1690, an event which is still celebrated by some in Northern Ireland today.","p":1,"o":[["The Battle of the Boyne",1],["The Battle of Killiecrankie",0],["The Battle of Culloden",0],["The Battle of Naseby",0]]},{"g":3650,"t":"Why was the revolution of 1688 called the 'Glorious Revolution'?","e":"This event was later called the 'Glorious Revolution' because there was no fighting in England and because it guaranteed the power of Parliament, ending the threat of a monarch ruling on his or her own as he or she wished.","p":1,"o":[["There was no fighting in England and it guaranteed the power of Parliament",1],["It restored the Catholic faith in England",0],["It gave every man the right to vote",0],["It united Scotland with England",0]]},{"g":3651,"t":"What happened to the MacDonalds of Glencoe?","e":"All Scottish clans were required formally to accept William as king by taking an oath. The MacDonalds of Glencoe were late in taking the oath and were all killed. The memory of this massacre meant some Scots distrusted the new government.","p":1,"o":[["They were all killed for being late in taking the oath to William",1],["They were exiled to France with James II",0],["They were pardoned by William",0],["They were given land in Ulster",0]]},{"g":3652,"t":"What were the supporters of James II known as?","e":"Some continued to believe that James was the rightful king, particularly in Scotland. Some joined him in exile in France; others were secret supporters. James' supporters became known as Jacobites.","p":1,"o":[["Jacobites",1],["Roundheads",0],["Whigs",0],["Chartists",0]]},{"g":3653,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"James II's elder daughter, Mary, was married to her cousin William of Orange, the Protestant ruler of the Netherlands. William took over the throne, becoming William III in England, Wales and Ireland, and William II of Scotland. He ruled jointly with Mary.","p":1,"o":[["William of Orange became William III in England and William II in Scotland",1],["William of Orange became William II in England and William III in Scotland",0]]}]},
    {"n":520,"name":"Toughest Test 20","q":[{"g":3654,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"In 1918, women over the age of 30 were given voting rights and the right to stand for Parliament, partly in recognition of the contribution women made to the war effort during the First World War. Shortly before Emmeline Pankhurst's death in 1928, women were given the right to vote at the age of 21, the same as men.","p":1,"o":[["In 1918 women over 30 were given voting rights, and in 1928 women could vote at 21",1],["In 1918 women over 21 were given voting rights, and in 1928 women could vote at 30",0]]},{"g":3655,"t":"The present voting age of 18 was set in which year?","e":"The UK has had a fully democratic voting system since 1928. The present voting age of 18 was set in 1969 and, with a few exceptions, all UK-born and naturalised adult citizens have the right to vote.","p":2,"o":[["1969",1],["1928",0],["1918",0],["1832",0]]},{"g":3656,"t":"Which TWO forms of protest did the suffragettes of the WSPU use?","e":"Emmeline Pankhurst co-founded the Women's Social and Political Union in 1903. This was the first group whose members were called suffragettes. The group used civil disobedience as part of their protest, and many of the women went on hunger strike.","p":1,"o":[["Civil disobedience",1],["Hunger strikes",1],["Petitions presented to Parliament",0],["Standing for election as MPs",0]]},{"g":3657,"t":"Which group did Emmeline Pankhurst set up in 1889, and what did it fight for?","e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858. She set up the Women's Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women.","p":1,"o":[["The Women's Franchise League, fighting for the vote in local elections for married women",1],["The Women's Social and Political Union, fighting for the vote in local elections",0],["The Chartists, fighting for the working man's vote",0],["The Women's Franchise League, fighting for the vote in General Elections",0]]},{"g":3658,"t":"Before 1870, what happened to a woman's earnings and property when she married?","e":"In common with the rest of Europe, women in 19th century Britain had fewer rights than men. Until 1870, when a woman got married, her earnings, property and money automatically belonged to her husband. Acts of Parliament in 1870 and 1882 gave wives the right to keep their own earnings and property.","p":1,"o":[["They automatically belonged to her husband",1],["They were held in trust by the courts",0],["They passed to her eldest son",0],["They remained entirely her own",0]]},{"g":3659,"t":"What did the Reform Act of 1867 do?","e":"Campaigners, called the Chartists, presented petitions to Parliament. At first they seemed to be unsuccessful, but in 1867 there was another Reform Act. This created many more urban seats in Parliament and reduced the amount of property that people needed to have before they could vote.","p":1,"o":[["It created more urban seats and reduced the property needed to vote",1],["It gave the vote to all women over 30",0],["It abolished the pocket and rotten boroughs",0],["It made MPs paid for the first time",0]]},{"g":3660,"t":"What is democracy, according to the handbook?","e":"Democracy is a system of government where the whole adult population gets a say. This might be by direct voting or by choosing representatives to make decisions on their behalf.","p":2,"o":[["A system of government where the whole adult population gets a say",1],["A system where only property owners may vote",0],["A system where the monarch chooses the government",0],["A system where judges make the laws",0]]},{"g":3661,"t":"At the turn of the 19th century, who was able to vote in elections for members of Parliament?","e":"At the turn of the 19th century, Britain was not a democracy as we know it today. Although there were elections to select members of Parliament, only a small group of people could vote. They were men who were over 21 years of age and who owned a certain amount of property.","p":2,"o":[["Men over 21 who owned a certain amount of property",1],["All men over 21",0],["All adults over 21",0],["Men over 18 who owned property",0]]},{"g":3662,"t":"Which TWO changes did the Chartists want, besides the vote for every man?","e":"In the 1830s and 1840s, a group called the Chartists campaigned for reform. They wanted six changes: for every man to have the vote, elections every year, for all regions to be equal in the electoral system, secret ballots, for any man to be able to stand as an MP, and for MPs to be paid.","p":2,"o":[["Elections every year",1],["For all regions to be equal in the electoral system",1],["Votes for women at 21",0],["The abolition of the monarchy",0]]},{"g":3663,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nThe UK has had a fully democratic voting system since 1928.","e":"The UK has had a fully democratic voting system since 1928. The present voting age of 18 was set in 1969 and, with a few exceptions, all UK-born and naturalised adult citizens have the right to vote.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3664,"t":"At what age can a person drive a car, and at what age a moped?","e":"In the UK, you must be at least 17 years old to drive a car or motor cycle and you must have a driving licence to drive on public roads. You need to be at least 16 years old to ride a moped.","p":2,"o":[["17 for a car and 16 for a moped",1],["16 for a car and 17 for a moped",0],["18 for a car and 16 for a moped",0],["17 for a car and 17 for a moped",0]]},{"g":3665,"t":"Until what age is a driving licence valid, and how often must it be renewed after that?","e":"Drivers can use their driving licence until they are 70 years old. After that, the licence is valid for three years at a time.","p":2,"o":[["Until 70, then it is valid for three years at a time",1],["Until 75, then it is valid for three years at a time",0],["Until 70, then it is valid for one year at a time",0],["Until 65, then it is valid for five years at a time",0]]},{"g":3666,"t":"Which TWO tests must be passed to get a UK driving licence?","e":"To get a UK driving licence you must pass a driving test, which tests both your knowledge and your practical skills. You will need to pass a driving theory test, which consists of a multiple choice theory test and a hazard perception test, and a practical driving test.","p":2,"o":[["A driving theory test",1],["A practical driving test",1],["A medical examination",0],["An English language test",0]]},{"g":3667,"t":"In Northern Ireland, what must a newly qualified driver display, and for how long?","e":"In Northern Ireland, a newly qualified driver must display an 'R' plate, for restricted driver, for one year after passing the test.","p":2,"o":[["An 'R' plate, for one year",1],["An 'L' plate, for one year",0],["An 'R' plate, for three years",0],["An 'R' plate, for six months",0]]},{"g":3668,"t":"For how long may a driving licence from a country outside the EU, Iceland, Liechtenstein or Norway be used in the UK?","e":"If your driving licence is from a country in the European Union, Iceland, Liechtenstein or Norway, you can drive in the UK for as long as your licence is valid. If you have a licence from any other country, you may use it in the UK for up to 12 months.","p":2,"o":[["Up to 12 months",1],["Up to three years",0],["Up to six months",0],["For as long as the licence is valid",0]]},{"g":3669,"t":"At what age does a vehicle need an annual MOT test?","e":"If your vehicle is over three years old, you must take it for a Ministry of Transport (MOT) test every year. It is an offence not to have an MOT certificate if your vehicle is more than three years old.","p":2,"o":[["When it is over three years old",1],["When it is over one year old",0],["When it is over ten years old",0],["When it is over five years old",0]]},{"g":3670,"t":"Is the statement below TRUE or FALSE?\nIt is a serious criminal offence to drive without insurance.","e":"If you are resident in the UK, your car or motor cycle must be registered at the Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency. You must pay an annual road tax and you must also have valid motor insurance. It is a serious criminal offence to drive without insurance.","p":2,"o":[["TRUE",1],["FALSE",0]]},{"g":3671,"t":"Which TWO charities named in the handbook work with homeless people?","e":"They include charities working with older people, such as Age UK, with children, for example the NSPCC, and with the homeless, for example Crisis and Shelter.","p":2,"o":[["Crisis",1],["Shelter",1],["Age UK",0],["The PDSA",0]]},{"g":3672,"t":"Which charity named in the handbook works with animals?","e":"There are also medical research charities, for example Cancer Research UK, environmental charities, including the National Trust and Friends of the Earth, and charities working with animals, such as the People's Dispensary for Sick Animals (PDSA).","p":2,"o":[["The PDSA",1],["The NSPCC",0],["Age UK",0],["Cancer Research UK",0]]},{"g":3673,"t":"Which programme gives 16- and 17-year-olds the opportunity to enjoy outdoor activities and take part in a community project?","e":"There are many opportunities for young people to volunteer and receive accreditation which will help them to develop their skills. These include the National Citizen Service programme, which gives 16- and 17-year-olds the opportunity to enjoy outdoor activities, develop their skills and take part in a community project.","p":2,"o":[["The National Citizen Service",1],["The School Governors' One-Stop Shop",0],["The Duke of Edinburgh's Award",0],["The Citizens Advice Bureau",0]]},{"g":3674,"t":"Which TWO roles can you volunteer for with the police?","e":"You can volunteer with the police, and become a special constable or a lay, non-police, representative. You can also apply to become a magistrate.","p":2,"o":[["A special constable",1],["A lay representative",1],["A Chief Constable",0],["A Police and Crime Commissioner",0]]},{"g":3675,"t":"Which of these statements is correct?","e":"Volunteering is working for good causes without payment. There are many benefits to volunteering, such as meeting new people and helping make your community a better place. Some volunteer activities will give you a chance to practise your English or develop work skills.","p":2,"o":[["Volunteering is working for good causes without payment",1],["Volunteering is paid work carried out for a charity",0]]},{"g":3676,"t":"Which TWO of these are ways of being a good neighbour, according to the handbook?","e":"You can help prevent any problems and conflicts with your neighbours by respecting their privacy and limiting how much noise you make. Also try to keep your garden tidy, and only put your refuse bags and bins on the street or in communal areas if they are due to be collected.","p":2,"o":[["Respecting their privacy",1],["Limiting how much noise you make",1],["Feeding their pets without being asked",0],["Parking in front of their house",0]]},{"g":3677,"t":"Which of these is NOT listed in the handbook as a shared value and responsibility?","e":"These values and responsibilities include: to obey and respect the law; to be aware of the rights of others and respect those rights; to treat others with fairness; to behave responsibly; to help and protect your family; to respect and preserve the environment; to treat everyone equally; to work to provide for yourself and your family; to help others; and to vote in local and national government elections.","p":3,"o":[["To join a trade union",1],["To respect and preserve the environment",0],["To treat others with fairness",0],["To vote in local and national government elections",0]]}]}
  ],
});
